ven because the sum of even numbers is even.\n> Since $\\deg(v_7) + \\deg(v_9) + \\deg(v_{11}) = \\bbox[]{\\color{black}27}$, the complete expression on the left-hand side must be odd because the sum of an even number and an odd number is odd.\n> On the other hand, the right-hand side of our degree sum formula is even as it is a multiple of $2$.\n> Therefore, the graph described above cannot exist."] [:db/cas 17592186114945 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow (-1/2)} x^3$." "Find $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow (-1/2)} x^3.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186114955 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_\\limits{x\\,\\rightarrow \\,-4} \\sqrt{7}$." "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{x\\,\\rightarrow \\,-4} \\sqrt{7}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186114964 :question/problem "The figure below shows the graph of $y=f(x)$. Evaluate $\\lim_\\limits{x \\rightarrow \\,0} \\Bigl(-2.5f(x)\\Bigr)$.\n\n![](images/42/42ad0609c570bf56d583811e3a15efd3fa253977738afca69bbc4361ee421216.png)" "The figure below shows the graph of $y=f(x)$. Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{x \\rightarrow \\,0} \\Bigl(-2.5f(x)\\Bigr).\n$$\n\n![](images/42/42ad0609c570bf56d583811e3a15efd3fa253977738afca69bbc4361ee421216.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186114965 :question/problem "Given that $\\displaystyle \\lim_{y\\rightarrow \\sqrt{7}/2} Cy^2=14$, where $C$ is a real constant, what is the value of $C$?" "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{y\\rightarrow \\sqrt{7}/2} Cy^2=14,\n$$ where $C$ is a real constant, what is the value of $C$?"] [:db/cas 17592186114966 :question/problem "Calculate  $\\lim\\limits_{t \\to 0} \\mathbf{f}(t),$ given that \n\n$$\n\\mathbf f (t) = \\dfrac{1- e^{t}}{\\sin t} \\ \\mathbf {i}+ \\dfrac{\\sin t}{t+t^2} \\ \\mathbf {j} + \\dfrac{ e^{t} - e^{-t}}{\\sin t} \\ \\mathbf {k}.\n$$" "Calculate  $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{t \\to 0} \\mathbf{f}(t),\n$$ given that \n\n$$\n\\mathbf f (t) = \\dfrac{1- e^{t}}{\\sin t} \\ \\mathbf {i}+ \\dfrac{\\sin t}{t+t^2} \\ \\mathbf {j} + \\dfrac{ e^{t} - e^{-t}}{\\sin t} \\ \\mathbf {k}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186114967 :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim\\limits_{t \\to \\infty} \\| \\mathbf f(t) \\|$ if $\\mathbf f(t)  = \\left\\langle t^{-1}, \\: \\left(1+\\dfrac{3}{t}\\right)^t, \\: \\dfrac{t^2}{1-t^2} \\right\\rangle.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{t \\to \\infty} \\| \\mathbf f(t) \\|\n$$ if $$\n\\displaystyle \\mathbf f(t)  = \\left\\langle t^{-1}, \\: \\left(1+\\dfrac{3}{t}\\right)^t, \\: \\dfrac{t^2}{1-t^2} \\right\\rangle.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186114968 :question/problem "Find $\\displaystyle \\lim_{t \\to 0} \\big[ \\mathbf f(t) - 2 \\mathbf g(t) \\big]$ given that $\\mathbf f(t)$ and $\\mathbf g(t)$ are defined as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ {e}^{t} \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix},\\,g(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2t\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Find $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{t \\to 0} \\big[ \\mathbf f(t) - 2 \\mathbf g(t) \\big]\n$$ given that $\\mathbf f(t)$ and $\\mathbf g(t)$ are defined as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ {e}^{t} \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix},\\,g(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2t\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186114969 :question/problem "Find $\\displaystyle \\lim_{t \\to 2} \\left[\\mathbf f(t) \\cdot \\mathbf g(t)\\right]$ given that $\\mathbf f(t)$ and $\\mathbf g(t)$ are defined as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,i+t\\,j+{t}^{2}\\,k \\\\ g(t) & =(2-t)\\,i+\\frac{6}{t}\\,j-4\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Find $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{t \\to 2} \\left[\\mathbf f(t) \\cdot \\mathbf g(t)\\right]\n$$ given that $\\mathbf f(t)$ and $\\mathbf g(t)$ are defined as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,i+t\\,j+{t}^{2}\\,k \\\\ g(t) & =(2-t)\\,i+\\frac{6}{t}\\,j-4\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186114974 :question/worked-solution "Let's compute the limit of $\\mathbf f (t)$ as $t$ approaches $0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t) & =⟨{lim}_{t\\to 0}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,{lim}_{t\\to 0}({t}^{2}+1),\\,{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0,\\,{0}^{2}+1,\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,1,0⟩ \\\\  & =f(0)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\displaystyle \\lim_{t \\rightarrow t_0} \\mathbf f (t) = \\mathbf f (t_0)$ and therefore $\\mathbf f (t)$ is continuous at $t=0.$" "Let's compute the limit of $\\mathbf f (t)$ as $t$ approaches $0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t) & =⟨{lim}_{t\\to 0}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,{lim}_{t\\to 0}({t}^{2}+1),\\,{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0,\\,{0}^{2}+1,\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,1,0⟩ \\\\  & =f(0)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{t \\rightarrow t_0} \\mathbf f (t) = \\mathbf f (t_0)\n$$ and therefore $\\mathbf f (t)$ is continuous at $t=0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186114976 :question/problem "Is the vector function $\\mathbf{f}(t) = \\left\\langle 2e^t, \\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{t-3}, \\: \\sqrt{t} \\right\\rangle$ differentiable with respect to $t$ on $t \\in [0, 2]?$" "Is the vector function $$\n\\displaystyle \\mathbf{f}(t) = \\left\\langle 2e^t, \\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{t-3}, \\: \\sqrt{t} \\right\\rangle\n$$ differentiable with respect to $t$ on $t \\in [0, 2]?$"] [:db/cas 17592186114998 :question/worked-solution "Let's recall some of the basic properties of $y=\\sec x$:\n\n- The domain of the function is almost $x\\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$ except for the points where the function is undefined, namely $x=\\pm\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},\\pm\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2},\\dots$\n- The range of the function is $y\\in (-\\infty,-1] \\cup [1, +\\infty)$.\n- The period of the function is $2\\pi$.\n- It has no zeros and no global extrema.\n- The branches of the function alternate between positive and negative, with a positive branch in $\\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$.\n- The graph has vertical asymptotes at the points not in the domain: $x=\\pm\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},\\pm\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2},\\dots$.\n\nOf the given plots, the only one that has all of these properties is I.\n\n![](images/37/37725a42df9b709dbef121957bc5d177e50ebb682e6bc97bbff8373768f7be11.png)" "Let's recall some of the basic properties of $y=\\sec x$:\n\n- The domain of the function is almost $x\\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$ except for the points where the function is undefined, namely $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\pm\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},\\pm\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2},\\dots\n$$\n- The range of the function is $y\\in (-\\infty,-1] \\cup [1, +\\infty)$.\n- The period of the function is $2\\pi$.\n- It has no zeros and no global extrema.\n- The branches of the function alternate between positive and negative, with a positive branch in $$\n\\displaystyle \\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right).\n$$\n- The graph has vertical asymptotes at the points not in the domain: $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\pm\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},\\pm\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2},\\dots.\n$$\n\nOf the given plots, the only one that has all of these properties is I.\n\n![](images/37/37725a42df9b709dbef121957bc5d177e50ebb682e6bc97bbff8373768f7be11.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186115012 :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\csc {x} \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+4 & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =-4 \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\csc{x} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin{x}}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\sin{x} = \\dfrac{1}{(-2)} = -\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx = \\arcsin\\left(-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right) = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nThis value is outside the required domain. To get a value inside the required domain, we add $2\\pi.$\n\n$$\nx_1 = x+2\\pi = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{6} + 2\\pi = \\dfrac{11\\pi}6.\n$$\n\nSo, our first solution is $x_1 = \\dfrac{11\\pi}{6}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the  fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of $\\theta_R = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}.$ Since $\\sin{x}<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle $\\theta_R =\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}.$\n\n![](images/85/85b7b73fa793153cfedb2ffdb6825edd0845f93dbb472404862a78476a56fc35.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x_2$ lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo get the remaining solutions in the given interval, we need to find angles that are coterminal with $x_1$ or $x_2.$ To do this, we add integer multiples of $2\\pi$ until all solutions are found:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{3} & ={x}_{1}+2\\pi =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}+2\\pi =\\frac{23\\pi }{6} \\\\ {x}_{4} & ={x}_{2}+2\\pi =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}+2\\pi =\\frac{19\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\dfrac{23\\pi}{6}$ is outside the required domain, so we ignore it.\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=\\dfrac{7\\pi}{6},\\dfrac{11\\pi}{6}, \\dfrac{19\\pi}{6}.$" "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\csc {x} \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+4 & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =-4 \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\csc{x} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin{x}}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\sin{x} = \\dfrac{1}{(-2)} = -\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx = \\arcsin\\left(-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right) = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nThis value is outside the required domain. To get a value inside the required domain, we add $2\\pi.$\n\n$$\nx_1 = x+2\\pi = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{6} + 2\\pi = \\dfrac{11\\pi}6.\n$$\n\nSo, our first solution is $x_1 = \\dfrac{11\\pi}{6}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the  fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of $\\theta_R = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}.$ Since $\\sin{x}<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle $\\theta_R =\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}.$\n\n![](images/85/85b7b73fa793153cfedb2ffdb6825edd0845f93dbb472404862a78476a56fc35.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x_2$ lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo get the remaining solutions in the given interval, we need to find angles that are coterminal with $x_1$ or $x_2.$ To do this, we add integer multiples of $2\\pi$ until all solutions are found:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{3} & ={x}_{1}+2\\pi =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}+2\\pi =\\frac{23\\pi }{6} \\\\ {x}_{4} & ={x}_{2}+2\\pi =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}+2\\pi =\\frac{19\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\dfrac{23\\pi}{6}$ is outside the required domain, so we ignore it.\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\dfrac{7\\pi}{6},\\dfrac{11\\pi}{6}, \\dfrac{19\\pi}{6}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186115015 :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_\\limits{x\\to \\pi/8} \\left(1 + 2\\sin \\left(2x\\right) \\right)^2$." "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{x\\to \\pi/8} \\left(1 + 2\\sin \\left(2x\\right) \\right)^2.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186115016 :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_\\limits{x \\rightarrow \\infty} \\sin\\left(2x\\right)$." "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{x \\rightarrow \\infty} \\sin\\left(2x\\right).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186115017 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\sqrt{\\pi}/2}\\tan\\left(2x^2-\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)$." "Find $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\sqrt{\\pi}/2}\\tan\\left(2x^2-\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186115018 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_\\limits {x \\to (-\\pi/6)^{-}} \\tan\\left(x-\\dfrac\\pi 3 \\right)$." "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits {x \\to (-\\pi/6)^{-}} \\tan\\left(x-\\dfrac\\pi 3 \\right).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186115032 :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^{2} 9 x^2 \\, \\textrm d x .$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^{2} 9 x^2 \\, \\textrm d x .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186115039 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\pi/4} (\\sec x \\tan x - \\cos x)\\, \\textrm d x.$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\pi/4} (\\sec x \\tan x - \\cos x)\\, \\textrm d x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186115041 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^2(2x^3 - 5)\\, \\textrm d x.$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^2(2x^3 - 5)\\, \\textrm d x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186115047 :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{4} \\left(\\sqrt x - \\dfrac 2 {x^2}\\right) \\, \\textrm d x .$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{4} \\left(\\sqrt x - \\dfrac 2 {x^2}\\right) \\, \\textrm d x .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186115049 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to-\\infty} -3\\left(\\dfrac 1 2\\right)^x.$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to-\\infty} -3\\left(\\dfrac 1 2\\right)^x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186115061 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to 1} (3^{2x} + 2^{3x} + 1).$" "Find $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{x \\to 1} (3^{2x} + 2^{3x} + 1).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186115069 :question/problem "Compute $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} -5 \\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^{1+x}.$" "Compute $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} -5 \\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^{1+x}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186115072 :question/problem "Is \n$\\begin{aligned}E=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}$ \nan elementary matrix?" "Is \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{aligned}E=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$ \nan elementary matrix?"] [:db/cas 17592186115128 :question/problem "Given a data set with $12$ data points and $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^{12} x_i = 96,$ compute $\\overline{x}.$" "Given a data set with $12$ data points and $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^{12} x_i = 96,\n$$ compute $\\overline{x}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186115128 :question/worked-solution "If we have a data set that consists of the points $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n,$ then the mean is denoted by $\\overline{x}$ and is given by the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\overline{x} = \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum_{i=1}^n x_i\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=12$ and $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^{12} x_i = 96,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot 96=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "If we have a data set that consists of the points $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n,$ then the mean is denoted by $\\overline{x}$ and is given by the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\overline{x} = \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum_{i=1}^n x_i\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=12$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^{12} x_i = 96,\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot 96=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186115133 :question/worked-solution "If we have a data set that consists of the points $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n,$ then the variance of the data set is given by \n$$\n\\sigma^2=  \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})^2,\n$$\nwhere $\\overline{x} = \\dfrac{1}{n} \\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^n x_i$ is the mean of the data set.\n\nFirst, we need to compute the mean. There are $n=5$ points in our data set, so the mean is\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle \\overline{x} & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{n}} \\sum _{i = 1}^{n} {x}_{i} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{5}} \\cdot  ( 5 + 13 + 6 + 8 + 3 ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{5}} \\cdot  35 \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 7.\\end{array}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the variance is\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle {\\sigma }^{2} & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{n}} \\sum _{i = 1}^{n} ( {x}_{i} - \\overline{x} {)}^{2} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{5}} [ ( 5 - 7 {)}^{2} + ( 13 - 7 {)}^{2} + ( 6 - 7 {)}^{2} + ( 8 - 7 {)}^{2} + ( 3 - 7 {)}^{2} ] \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{5}} [ 4 + 36 + 1 + 1 + 16 ] \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{58}{5}} .\\end{array}\n$$" "If we have a data set that consists of the points $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n,$ then the variance of the data set is given by \n$$\n\\sigma^2=  \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})^2,\n$$\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle \\overline{x} = \\dfrac{1}{n} \\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^n x_i\n$$ is the mean of the data set.\n\nFirst, we need to compute the mean. There are $n=5$ points in our data set, so the mean is\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle \\overline{x} & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{n}} \\sum _{i = 1}^{n} {x}_{i} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{5}} \\cdot  ( 5 + 13 + 6 + 8 + 3 ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{5}} \\cdot  35 \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 7.\\end{array}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the variance is\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle {\\sigma }^{2} & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{n}} \\sum _{i = 1}^{n} ( {x}_{i} - \\overline{x} {)}^{2} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{5}} [ ( 5 - 7 {)}^{2} + ( 13 - 7 {)}^{2} + ( 6 - 7 {)}^{2} + ( 8 - 7 {)}^{2} + ( 3 - 7 {)}^{2} ] \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{5}} [ 4 + 36 + 1 + 1 + 16 ] \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{58}{5}} .\\end{array}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186115136 :question/worked-solution "If we have a data set that consists of the points $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n,$ then the variance of the data set is given by \n\n$$\n\\sigma^2 = \\overline{x^2} - (\\overline{x})^2,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overline{x}$ is the mean of the data set and $\\overline{x^2}$ is the mean of the squares of the points in the data set.\n\nFirst, let's compute $\\overline{x}.$ There are $n=8$ points in our data set, and we know that $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^{8} x_i = 40,$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{8}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot 40 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\overline{x^2}.$ We know that $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^{8} x_i^2 = 320,$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{8}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot 320 \\\\  & =40.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-(\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =40-{(5)}^{2} \\\\  & =40-25 \\\\  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "If we have a data set that consists of the points $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n,$ then the variance of the data set is given by \n\n$$\n\\sigma^2 = \\overline{x^2} - (\\overline{x})^2,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overline{x}$ is the mean of the data set and $\\overline{x^2}$ is the mean of the squares of the points in the data set.\n\nFirst, let's compute $\\overline{x}.$ There are $n=8$ points in our data set, and we know that $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^{8} x_i = 40,\n$$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{8}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot 40 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\overline{x^2}.$ We know that $$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^{8} x_i^2 = 320,\n$$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{8}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot 320 \\\\  & =40.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-(\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =40-{(5)}^{2} \\\\  & =40-25 \\\\  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186115486 :question/problem "Find \n$|\\begin{aligned}1 & 9 & 0 \\\\ d & e & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}|$\ngiven that \n$|\\begin{aligned}1 & 9 \\\\ d & e\\end{aligned}|=9.$" "Find \n$$\n\\displaystyle |\\begin{aligned}1 & 9 & 0 \\\\ d & e & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}|\n$$\ngiven that \n$$\n\\displaystyle |\\begin{aligned}1 & 9 \\\\ d & e\\end{aligned}|=9.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186115492 :question/problem "Represent the following system of equations using matrices:\n$\\begin{array}{r}\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}\\displaystyle 3 x + 5 y = 4 \\\\ \\displaystyle x - 7 y = - 4\\end{array} \\right. \\end{array}$" "Represent the following system of equations using matrices:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{array}{r}\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}\\displaystyle 3 x + 5 y = 4 \\\\ \\displaystyle x - 7 y = - 4\\end{array} \\right. \\end{array}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186115493 :question/problem "Represent the following system of equations using matrices:\n$\\begin{array}{r}\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}\\displaystyle 2 y = 3 \\\\ \\displaystyle x = 0\\end{array} \\right. \\end{array}$" "Represent the following system of equations using matrices:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{array}{r}\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}\\displaystyle 2 y = 3 \\\\ \\displaystyle x = 0\\end{array} \\right. \\end{array}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186115513 :question/problem "Find the domain of the vector-valued function $f(s)=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+s),\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-s),\\,\\frac{1}{1+s}⟩.$" "Find the domain of the vector-valued function $$\n\\displaystyle f(s)=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+s),\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-s),\\,\\frac{1}{1+s}⟩.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186115525 :question/problem "What is $\\displaystyle \\int_{π/6}^{π/4}(\\sec^{2} x - \\csc^{2} x)dx$?" "What is $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{π/6}^{π/4}(\\sec^{2} x - \\csc^{2} x)dx\n$$?"] [:db/cas 17592186115538 :question/problem "What is $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{9}(\\sqrt{x} - 1)dx$?" "What is $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{9}(\\sqrt{x} - 1)dx\n$$?"] [:db/cas 17592186115542 :question/problem "What is $\\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^{2}(6x^{2} + 3x)