athbf{y} = \\mathbf{b} \\\\ U \\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{y}\\end{array} \\right.$\n\nis equivalent to $Ax = b$." "Consider the matrix $L$ and the vector $b$, given by\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle L = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}, b = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf $A = LU$ is the LU factorization of some $3 \\cdot 3$ matrix $A$, find the vector $y$ such that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}L \\mathbf{y} = \\mathbf{b} \\\\ U \\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{y}\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\nis equivalent to $Ax = b$."] [:db/cas 17592186120193 :question/problem "Given that\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 5 & 10 & 9\\end{aligned}]$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then ${l}_{31}\\cdot {u}_{33}=$" "Given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 5 & 10 & 9\\end{aligned}]\n$$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then ${l}_{31}\\cdot {u}_{33}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186120203 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $L$ and the vector $b$, given by\n\n$L = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -12 & 1\\end{bmatrix}, b = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -25\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nIf $A = LU$ is the LU factorization of some $2 \\cdot 2$ matrix $A$, find the vector $y$ such that\n\n$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}L \\mathbf{y} = \\mathbf{b} \\\\ U \\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{y}\\end{array} \\right.$\nis equivalent to $Ax = b$." "Consider the matrix $L$ and the vector $b$, given by\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle L = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -12 & 1\\end{bmatrix}, b = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -25\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf $A = LU$ is the LU factorization of some $2 \\cdot 2$ matrix $A$, find the vector $y$ such that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}L \\mathbf{y} = \\mathbf{b} \\\\ U \\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{y}\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\nis equivalent to $Ax = b$."] [:db/cas 17592186120207 :question/problem "Calculate the determinant of the matrix\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 8 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$" "Calculate the determinant of the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 8 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186120209 :question/problem "$\\underset{A}{\\underbrace{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}4 & 2 & - 2 \\\\ 16 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 4 & - 2 & 8\\end{array} \\right]}} = \\underset{L}{\\underbrace{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{array} \\right]}} \\cdot  \\underset{U}{\\underbrace{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}4 & 2 & - 2 \\\\ 0 & - 4 & 14 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\boxed{\\phantom{\\hspace{0.167em} N \\hspace{0.167em}}}\\end{array} \\right]}} .$\n\nConsider the LU factorization of the matrix $A$ above. What is the value of $u_{33}$ in the matrix $U$?" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\underset{A}{\\underbrace{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}4 & 2 & - 2 \\\\ 16 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 4 & - 2 & 8\\end{array} \\right]}} = \\underset{L}{\\underbrace{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{array} \\right]}} \\cdot  \\underset{U}{\\underbrace{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}4 & 2 & - 2 \\\\ 0 & - 4 & 14 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\boxed{\\phantom{\\hspace{0.167em} N \\hspace{0.167em}}}\\end{array} \\right]}} .\n$$\n\nConsider the LU factorization of the matrix $A$ above. What is the value of $u_{33}$ in the matrix $U$?"] [:db/cas 17592186120230 :question/worked-solution "For a curve $r(t)=x(t)i+y(t)j+z(t)k$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-t}p(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-t})p(t)+{e}^{-t}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(p(t)) \\\\  & =-{e}^{-t}p(t)+{e}^{-t}{p}^{′}(t) \\\\ {r}^{′}(0) & =-{e}^{0}p(0)+{e}^{0}{p}^{′}(0) \\\\  & =-(\\,i+\\,j+k)+(\\,i-\\,j) \\\\  & =-2j-k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{(0)^{2}+(-2)^{2}+(1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0+4+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${r}^{′}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(0) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(0)}{‖{r}^{′}(0)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}(-2j-k) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}j-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit tangent vector to the curve at the point that corresponds to $t=0$ is $T(0)=-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}j-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}k.$" "For a curve $r(t)=x(t)i+y(t)j+z(t)k$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-t}p(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-t})p(t)+{e}^{-t}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(p(t)) \\\\  & =-{e}^{-t}p(t)+{e}^{-t}{p}^{′}(t) \\\\ {r}^{′}(0) & =-{e}^{0}p(0)+{e}^{0}{p}^{′}(0) \\\\  & =-(\\,i+\\,j+k)+(\\,i-\\,j) \\\\  & =-2j-k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{(0)^{2}+(-2)^{2}+(1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0+4+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${r}^{′}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(0) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(0)}{‖{r}^{′}(0)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}(-2j-k) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}j-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit tangent vector to the curve at the point that corresponds to $t=0$ is $$\n\\displaystyle T(0)=-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}j-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}k.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186120233 :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(0,2,1)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n$\\frac{x-1}{-2}=\\frac{y-3}{1}=\\frac{z}{5}.$" "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(0,2,1)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{x-1}{-2}=\\frac{y-3}{1}=\\frac{z}{5}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186120237 :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(8,3,-1)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n$\\frac{x+2}{4}=\\frac{y-1}{3}=\\frac{z-2}{-1}.$" "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(8,3,-1)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{x+2}{4}=\\frac{y-1}{3}=\\frac{z-2}{-1}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186120245 :question/problem "Find the vector equation of the line $\\frac{x - 4}{2} = \\frac{y - 2}{-3} = \\frac{z + 6}{8}$." "Find the vector equation of the line $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{x - 4}{2} = \\frac{y - 2}{-3} = \\frac{z + 6}{8}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186120258 :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $9\\,Ω,$ and an inductor of inductance $1\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=9\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=9I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=1\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=1\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}=\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)=9I+\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+9I=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+9{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-9t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $3t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $3t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))+9(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ (-3\\alpha +9\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+(9\\alpha +3\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) & =8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3\\alpha +9\\beta &=8\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) \\\\ 9\\alpha +3\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{4}{15},$ $\\beta =\\frac{4}{5}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{4}{15}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-9t}-\\frac{4}{15}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-9t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{4}{15}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{4}{15}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{\\left(-\\frac{4}{15}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}^{2}}≈0.84\\,\\text{A},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $9\\,Ω,$ and an inductor of inductance $1\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=9\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=9I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=1\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=1\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}=\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)=9I+\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+9I=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+9{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-9t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $3t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $3t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))+9(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ (-3\\alpha +9\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+(9\\alpha +3\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) & =8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3\\alpha +9\\beta &=8\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) \\\\ 9\\alpha +3\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{4}{15},$ $\\beta =\\frac{4}{5}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{4}{15}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-9t}-\\frac{4}{15}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-9t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function $$\n\\displaystyle {I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{4}{15}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\n$$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{4}{15}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{\\left(-\\frac{4}{15}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}^{2}}≈0.84\\,\\text{A},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186120275 :question/problem "Find the arc length of the curve $f(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}{t}^{2}⟩$\nfrom $t=1$ to $t=3.$" "Find the arc length of the curve $$\n\\displaystyle f(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}{t}^{2}⟩\n$$\nfrom $t=1$ to $t=3.$"] [:db/cas 17592186120277 :question/problem "Find a normal vector to the plane\n$r = \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix} + t\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} + s\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}, t, s ∈ (- ∞, ∞)$." "Find a normal vector to the plane\n$$\n\\displaystyle r = \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix} + t\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} + s\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}, t, s ∈ (- ∞, ∞).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186120291 :question/problem "Find the equation of the osculating plane (in parametric form) for the curve $r(t)$ at the point where $t = π$, given that\n\n$r(t) = 5\\cos ti + 3\\sin tj + 4\\sin tk, T(π) =-\\frac{3}{5}j - \\frac{4}{5}k, N(π) = i$.\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." "Find the equation of the osculating plane (in parametric form) for the curve $r(t)$ at the point where $t = π$, given that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle r(t) = 5\\cos ti + 3\\sin tj + 4\\sin tk, T(π) =-\\frac{3}{5}j - \\frac{4}{5}k, N(π) = i.\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively."] [:db/cas 17592186120305 :question/problem "Find the curvature at an arbitrary point on the curve $r(t)$ given that its derivative $r^{′}(t)$ and the derivative of the corresponding unit tangent $T^{′}(t)$ are as follows:\n$r^{′}(t) = ⟨ - 4\\sin t, 4\\cos t, 3⟩, T^{′}(t) = ⟨ - \\frac{4}{5}\\cos t,-\\frac{4}{5}\\sin t, 0⟩$" "Find the curvature at an arbitrary point on the curve $r(t)$ given that its derivative $r^{′}(t)$ and the derivative of the corresponding unit tangent $T^{′}(t)$ are as follows:\n$$\n\\displaystyle r^{′}(t) = ⟨ - 4\\sin t, 4\\cos t, 3⟩, T^{′}(t) = ⟨ - \\frac{4}{5}\\cos t,-\\frac{4}{5}\\sin t, 0⟩\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186120306 :question/worked-solution "By definition, $u\\in Π$ if there exist $t$ and $s$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sb+tc & =u \\\\ s\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -8 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-s+t &=1 \\\\ 3s-5t &=-8 \\\\ 5s-7t &=-10\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -5 & -8 \\\\ 5 & -7 & -10\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -5 \\\\ 5 & -7 & -10\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+5{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -5 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-s+t &=1 \\\\ -2t &=-5 \\\\ 0 &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n-2t=-5\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-s+t & =1 \\\\ -s+\\frac{5}{2} & =1 \\\\ -s & =-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ s & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we found a solution $s=\\frac{3}{2},\\,t=\\frac{5}{2},$ we conclude that $u$ does indeed lie on the plane $Π.$ In fact, we have\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{3}{2}b+\\frac{5}{2}c.\n$$\n\nFinally, $s\\cdot t=\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}=\\frac{15}{4}.$" "By definition, $u\\in Π$ if there exist $t$ and $s$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sb+tc & =u \\\\ s\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -8 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-s+t &=1 \\\\ 3s-5t &=-8 \\\\ 5s-7t &=-10\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system above, we set up and row reduce an augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -5 & -8 \\\\ 5 & -7 & -10\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -5 \\\\ 5 & -7 & -10\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+5{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -5 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-s+t &=1 \\\\ -2t &=-5 \\\\ 0 &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n-2t=-5\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-s+t & =1 \\\\ -s+\\frac{5}{2} & =1 \\\\ -s & =-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ s & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we found a solution $s=\\frac{3}{2},\\,t=\\frac{5}{2},$ we conclude that $u$ does indeed lie on the plane $Π.$ In fact, we have\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{3}{2}b+\\frac{5}{2}c.\n$$\n\nFinally, $$\n\\displaystyle s\\cdot t=\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}=\\frac{15}{4}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186120312 :question/problem "Find $q$, if $7a_{1} + qa_{2} = b$ and\n$a_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}, a_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}, b = \\begin{bmatrix}32 \\\\ 17\\end{bmatrix}$." "Find $q$, if $7a_{1} + qa_{2} = b$ and\n$$\n\\displaystyle a_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}, a_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}, b = \\begin{bmatrix}32 \\\\ 17\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186120313 :question/problem "The matrix $B$ and the vector $b$ are given below. Determine if $b ∈ Col(B)$. In the affirmative case, find the value of $x_{1} \\cdot x_{2}$, where $x_{1}$ and $x_{2}$ are real numbers such that $b = x_{1}b_{1} + x_{2}b_{2}$, and $b_{1}$, $b_{2}$ are the respective columns of $B$.\n\n$B = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}, b = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}$" "The matrix $B$ and the vector $b$ are given below. Determine if $b ∈ Col(B)$. In the affirmative case, find the value of $x_{1} \\cdot x_{2}$, where $x_{1}$ and $x_{2}$ are real numbers such that $b = x_{1}b_{1} + x_{2}b_{2}$, and $b_{1}$, $b_{2}$ are the respective columns of $B$.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle B = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}, b = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186120314 :question/problem "The matrix $C$ and the vector $b$ are given below.\nDetermine if $b ∈ Col(C)$.\nIn the affirmative case, find the value of $x_{1} \\cdot x_{2} \\cdot x_{3}$, where $x_{1}, x_{2}$ and $x_{3}$ are real numbers such that $b = x_{1}c_{1} + x_{2}c_{2} + x_{3}c_{3}$, and $c_{1}$, $c_{2}$, and $c_{3}$ are the respective columns of $C$.\n\n$C = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}, b = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}$" "The matrix $C$ and the vector $b$ are given below.\nDetermine if $b ∈ Col(C)$.\nIn the affirmative case, find the value of $x_{1} \\cdot x_{2} \\cdot x_{3}$, where $x_{1}, x_{2}$ and $x_{3}$ are real numbers such that $b = x_{1}c_{1} + x_{2}c_{2} + x_{3}c_{3}$, and $c_{1}$, $c_{2}$, and $c_{3}$ are the respective columns of $C$.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle C = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}, b = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186120316 :question/problem "Consider the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by arc length $s$. Find the curvature at the point where $s = \\frac{5π}{2}$ if the unit tangent vector of the curve is given by\n$T(s) = ⟨\\frac{3}{5},-\\frac{4}{5}\\sin \\left(\\frac{2s}{5}\\right), \\frac{4}{5}\\cos \\left(\\frac{2s}{5}\\right)⟩$." "Consider the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by arc length $s$. Find the curvature at the point where $s = \\frac{5π}{2}$ if the unit tangent vector of the curve is given by\n$$\n\\displaystyle T(s) = ⟨\\frac{3}{5},-\\frac{4}{5}\\sin \\left(\\frac{2s}{5}\\right), \\frac{4}{5}\\cos \\left(\\frac{2s}{5}\\right)⟩.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186120320 :question/problem "The set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be ${a}_{2}$ if\n${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]?$" "The set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be ${a}_{2}$ if\n$$\n\\displaystyle {a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186120321 :question/problem "The set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be ${a}_{2}$ if\n${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}?$" "The set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be ${a}_{2}$ if\n$$\n\\displaystyle {a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186120323 :question/problem "The matrix $A$ and vectors $b_{1}$, $b_{2}$, $b_{3}$ are given below. Which of the vectors lie in $Null(A)$?\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}, b_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}, b_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}, b_{3} = \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$" "The matrix $A$ and vectors $b_{1}$, $b_{2}$, $b_{3}$ are given below. Which of the vectors lie in $Null(A)$?\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle A = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}, b_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}, b_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}, b_{3} = \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186120329 :question/problem "![](images/d3/d399a25c2a73883560aade5946007b21bf0b853e5d3ca262469df9b75e5843cd.png)\n\nConsider the RCL circuit shown above. Given that the charge $q(t)$ of the capacitor at time $t$ seconds is given by\n$q(t) = Ae^{-5t/2} + Be^{-t} + \\frac{P}{41}\\cos (2t) + \\frac{Q}{82}\\sin (2t)$\nwhere $A$, $B$, $P$, and $Q$ are constants, find the value of $P + Q$.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop $V_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_{C}$, where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" "![](images/d3/d399a25c2a73883560aade5946007b21bf0b853e5d3ca262469df9b75e5843cd.png)\n\nConsider the RCL circuit shown above. Given that the charge $q(t)$ of the capacitor at time $t$ seconds is given by\n$$\n\\displaystyle q(t) = Ae^{-5t/2} + Be^{-t} + \\frac{P}{41}\\cos (2t) + \\frac{Q}{82}\\sin (2t)\n$$\nwhere $A$, $B$, $P$, and $Q$ are constants, find the value of $P + Q$.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop $V_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_{C}$, where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*"] [:db/cas 17592186120330 :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $(39\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-65\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $70\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.01\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $10\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=70\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=70I=70\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.01}=100q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=10\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=10\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=10\\,\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(\\frac{dq}{dt}\\right)=10\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n39\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-65\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)=70\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+100q+10\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+7\\cdot \\frac{dq}{dt}+10q=\\frac{39}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{13}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{c}}{d{t}^{2}}+7\\cdot \\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+10{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-5t}+B{e}^{-2t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $3t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a sinusoidal with argument $3t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))+7(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))\\, &  \\\\ +10(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =\\frac{39}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{13}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ (-21\\alpha +\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+(\\alpha +21\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & =\\frac{39}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{13}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-21\\alpha +\\beta &=\\frac{39}{10}\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)) \\\\ \\alpha +21\\beta &=-\\frac{13}{2}\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{1}{5},$ $\\beta =-\\frac{3}{10}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-5t}+B{e}^{-2t}-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-5t}+B{e}^{-2t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$ is the *steady-state charge*. It governs the charge's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nq(t)≈{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state charge, in coulombs, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{\\left(-\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(-\\frac{3}{10}\\right)}^{2}}≈0.36,\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $(39\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-65\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $70\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.01\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $10\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=70\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=70I=70\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.01}=100q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=10\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=10\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=10\\,\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(\\frac{dq}{dt}\\right)=10\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,