ross the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=105\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=105I=105\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{(1/180)}=180q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=15\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=15\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=15\\,\\frac{d}{dt}\\left(\\frac{dq}{dt}\\right)=15\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n50\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)=105\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+180q+15\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+7\\cdot \\frac{dq}{dt}+12q=\\frac{10}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{c}}{d{t}^{2}}+7\\cdot \\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+12{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-4t}+B{e}^{-3t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $3t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a sinusoidal with argument $3t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{q}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(-9\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-9\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))+7(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))\\,\\, &  \\\\ +12(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =\\frac{10}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ (-21\\alpha +3\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+(3\\alpha +21\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & =\\frac{10}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-21\\alpha +3\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)) \\\\ 3\\alpha +21\\beta &=\\frac{10}{3}\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{1}{45},$ $\\beta =\\frac{7}{45}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{1}{45}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{7}{45}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-4t}+B{e}^{-3t}+\\frac{1}{45}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{7}{45}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this expression for $q(t),$ we obtain the following expression for $I$ in amperes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(A{e}^{-4t}+B{e}^{-3t}+\\frac{1}{45}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{7}{45}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) \\\\  & =-4A{e}^{-4t}-3B{e}^{-3t}-\\frac{1}{15}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+\\frac{7}{15}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the f