tries above $a_{42}$ equal to zero.*" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 & -7 & 10 \\\\ -9 & -1 & -6 & 9 \\\\ -7 & 2 & 3 & -11 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 4 & -7\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4 \\cdot 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3 \\cdot 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary row operations to make the entries above $a_{42}$ equal to zero.*"] [:db/cas 17592186120435 :question/problem "Given that \n$|\\begin{aligned}a+3 & -5 \\\\ b & 6\\end{aligned}|=-7$,\nfind \n$|\\begin{aligned}a+2 & -5 \\\\ b+4 & 6\\end{aligned}|.$" "Given that \n$$\n\\displaystyle |\\begin{aligned}a+3 & -5 \\\\ b & 6\\end{aligned}|=-7,\n$$\nfind \n$$\n\\displaystyle |\\begin{aligned}a+2 & -5 \\\\ b+4 & 6\\end{aligned}|.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186120439 :question/worked-solution "The determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =|\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =20-2 \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the value of $y$ is given by $y=\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D},$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{y} & =|\\begin{aligned}5 & k \\\\ -2 & -12\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D} \\\\ -3 & =\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}5 & k \\\\ -2 & -12\\end{aligned}|}{18} \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}5 & k \\\\ -2 & -12\\end{aligned}| & =-54\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is $|\\begin{aligned}5 & k \\\\ -2 & -12\\end{aligned}|=-54$ only." "The determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =|\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =20-2 \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the value of $y$ is given by $y=\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D},$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{y} & =|\\begin{aligned}5 & k \\\\ -2 & -12\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D} \\\\ -3 & =\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}5 & k \\\\ -2 & -12\\end{aligned}|}{18} \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}5 & k \\\\ -2 & -12\\end{aligned}| & =-54\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle |\\begin{aligned}5 & k \\\\ -2 & -12\\end{aligned}|=-54\n$$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186120444 :question/problem "What is $\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -4 & \\sqrt{2} & 0.5 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -2 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 5\\end{vmatrix}$?" "What is $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}2 & -4 & \\sqrt{2} & 0.5 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -2 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 5\\end{vmatrix}\n$$?"] [:db/cas 17592186120445 :question/problem "Find the determinant of the matrix\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$." "Find the determinant of the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186120446 :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,7x-2y &=10 \\\\ -2x+y &=8.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the unknown $x$ can be written as \n$x=\\frac{{D}_{x}}{|\\begin{aligned}7 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}|}$, where ${D}_{x}=$" "Consider the system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,7x-2y &=10 \\\\ -2x+y &=8.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the unknown $x$ can be written as \n$$\n\\displaystyle x=\\frac{{D}_{x}}{|\\begin{aligned}7 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}|},\n$$ where ${D}_{x}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186120447 :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-6x+8y &=-46 \\\\ 6x+5y &=20.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the unknown $y$ can be written as \n$y=\\frac{{D}_{y}}{|\\begin{aligned}-6 & 8 \\\\ 6 & 5\\end{aligned}|},$ \nwhere ${D}_{y}=$" "Consider the system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-6x+8y &=-46 \\\\ 6x+5y &=20.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the unknown $y$ can be written as \n$$\n\\displaystyle y=\\frac{{D}_{y}}{|\\begin{aligned}-6 & 8 \\\\ 6 & 5\\end{aligned}|},\n$$ \nwhere ${D}_{y}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186120448 :question/worked-solution "The determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ k & -4\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the value of $y$ is given by $y=\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D},$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{y} & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 10 \\\\ k & 12\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D} \\\\ 3 & =\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}2 & 10 \\\\ k & 12\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ k & -4\\end{aligned}|} \\\\ 3|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ k & -4\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 10 \\\\ k & 12\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is $3|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ k & -4\\end{aligned}|=|\\begin{aligned}2 & 10 \\\\ k & 12\\end{aligned}|$ only." "The determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ k & -4\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the value of $y$ is given by $y=\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D},$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{y} & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 10 \\\\ k & 12\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D} \\\\ 3 & =\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}2 & 10 \\\\ k & 12\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ k & -4\\end{aligned}|} \\\\ 3|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ k & -4\\end{aligned}| & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 10 \\\\ k & 12\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle 3|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ k & -4\\end{aligned}|=|\\begin{aligned}2 & 10 \\\\ k & 12\\end{aligned}|\n$$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186120449 :question/problem "Given that $A=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 10 & 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix},$ find $det(A).$" "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 10 & 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ find $det(A).$"] [:db/cas 17592186120450 :question/problem "Given that $A = \\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & -4 \\\\ -1 & 5\\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$, find $det(A)$." "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle A = \\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & -4 \\\\ -1 & 5\\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ find $det(A)$."] [:db/cas 17592186120457 :question/problem "$|\\begin{aligned}107 & 215 & 99 \\\\ 6 & 8 & 11 \\\\ 107 & 215 & 100\\end{aligned}|=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle |\\begin{aligned}107 & 215 & 99 \\\\ 6 & 8 & 11 \\\\ 107 & 215 & 100\\end{aligned}|=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186120492 :question/worked-solution "For a curve $r(t)=x(t)i+y(t)j+z(t)k$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,p(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot p(t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(p(t)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,p(t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{p}^{′}(t) \\\\ {r}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)p\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right){p}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i-\\,j+2k)+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+\\,j) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}(i+k)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)‖ & =\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}+(0)^{2}+(\\sqrt{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${r}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)}{‖{r}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}(i+k) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+k)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit tangent vector to the curve at the point that corresponds to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is\n $T\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+k).$" "For a curve $r(t)=x(t)i+y(t)j+z(t)k$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,p(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot p(t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(p(t)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,p(t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{p}^{′}(t) \\\\ {r}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)p\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right){p}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i-\\,j+2k)+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+\\,j) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}(i+k)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)‖ & =\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}+(0)^{2}+(\\sqrt{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${r}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)}{‖{r}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}(i+k) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+k)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit tangent vector to the curve at the point that corresponds to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is\n $$\n\\displaystyle T\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+k).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186120505 :question/problem "Find the cofactor of $d_{33}$ in $D = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -6 & 7 & 8 \\\\ 9 & 1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 5 & 6 & -7\\end{bmatrix}$." "Find the cofactor of $d_{33}$ in $$\n\\displaystyle D = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -6 & 7 & 8 \\\\ 9 & 1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 5 & 6 & -7\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186120509 :question/problem "What is the minor of ${x}_{33}$ in \n$X=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}?$" "What is the minor of ${x}_{33}$ in \n$$\n\\displaystyle X=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186120510 :question/problem "Find the minor of ${q}_{23}$ in \n$Q=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 9 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 5 & 8 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Find the minor of ${q}_{23}$ in \n$$\n\\displaystyle Q=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 9 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 5 & 8 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186120519 :question/problem "Given that \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 10\\end{bmatrix},$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=$" "Given that \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 10\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186120523 :question/problem "The vectors $a_{1}$, $a_{2}$, and $a_{3}$ are given below. Determine whether $B = {a_{1}, a_{2}}$ is a basis of $Span{a_{1}, a_{2}, a_{3}}$. If that is the case, find $x_{1} \\cdot x_{2}$, where $a_{3} = x_{1}a_{1} + x_{2}a_{2}$.\n$a_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}, a_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}, a_{3} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$" "The vectors $a_{1}$, $a_{2}$, and $a_{3}$ are given below. Determine whether $B = {a_{1}, a_{2}}$ is a basis of $Span{a_{1}, a_{2}, a_{3}}$. If that is the case, find $x_{1} \\cdot x_{2}$, where $a_{3} = x_{1}a_{1} + x_{2}a_{2}$.\n$$\n\\displaystyle a_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}, a_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}, a_{3} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186120526 :question/problem "Find the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B = {b_{1}, b_{2}, b_{3}}$, where\n$b_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, b_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}, b_{3} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}, x = \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$.\nIf $[x]_{B} = \\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ x_{3}\\end{bmatrix}$ then what is the value of $x_{1}$?" "Find the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B = {b_{1}, b_{2}, b_{3}}$, where\n$$\n\\displaystyle b_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, b_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}, b_{3} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}, x = \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nIf $[x]_{B} = \\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ x_{3}\\end{bmatrix}$ then what is the value of $x_{1}$?"] [:db/cas 17592186120529 :question/problem "$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\})=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\})=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186120529 :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, our matrix is already in a row echelon form.\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, $2$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore,\n$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\})=2.$" "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, our matrix is already in a row echelon form.\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, $2$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore,\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\})=2.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186120554 :question/problem "Calculate\n$\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}[2f(t) - g(t)]dt$\ngiven that\n$\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}f(t)dt = ⟨1,-1, 1⟩$\nand\n$\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}g(t)dt = ⟨ - 3,-2, 1⟩$." "Calculate\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}[2f(t) - g(t)]dt\n$$\ngiven that\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}f(t)dt = ⟨1,-1, 1⟩\n$$\nand\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}g(t)dt = ⟨ - 3,-2, 1⟩.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186120555 :question/problem "Calculate \n${\\int }_{1}^{2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\ngiven that\n${\\int }_{1}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨-2,\\,3,\\,4⟩$\nand\n${\\int }_{2}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨8,\\,-6,\\,15⟩.$" "Calculate \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\ngiven that\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨-2,\\,3,\\,4⟩\n$$\nand\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{2}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨8,\\,-6,\\,15⟩.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186120556 :question/problem "Calculate \n${\\int }_{0}^{1}f(t)+⟨1,\\,1,\\,1⟩\\,\\text{d}t$\ngiven that\n${\\int }_{1}^{0}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨-2,\\,3,\\,4⟩.$" "Calculate \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}f(t)+⟨1,\\,1,\\,1⟩\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\ngiven that\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{0}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨-2,\\,3,\\,4⟩.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186120557 :question/problem "Calculate \n${\\int }_{0}^{1}2f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\ngiven that\n${\\int }_{1}^{0}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨1,\\,-3,\\,2⟩.$" "Calculate \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}2f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\ngiven that\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{0}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=⟨1,\\,-3,\\,2⟩.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186120983 :tutorial/content "In many cases, the EMF produced by a power source is sinusoidal, not constant. Such a power source is shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/43/4398148f91eda2bae1392f79e868e10c8967ecc23ca9f26b791bc1fbacc45ff9.png)\n\nIn a first-order linear RL circuit driven by a sinusoidal voltage, the solution consists of a transient current that decays over time and a steady-state current that oscillates with the same frequency as the input and is independent of the initial condition.\n\nFor example, let's find the amplitude of the steady-state current in the RL circuit shown below.\n\n![](images/59/59fd8a8db1a227a578670d2457011275446c87ba218686f8d1984f4a73a8cfa6.png)\n\nThe diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $3\\sin(4t)\\,\\textrm{V},$ a resistor of resistance $7\\,\\Omega,$ and an inductor of inductance $1\\,\\textrm{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R = 7\\,\\Omega.$\n\n$$\nV_R = 7I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L = 1\\,\\textrm{H}.$\n\n$$\nV_L = 1\\cdot\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n3\\sin(4t) = 7I + 1\\cdot\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 7I = 3\\sin(4t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution $I_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I_c}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 7I_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\nI_c(t) = Ae^{-7t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $4t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $4t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\nI_p(t) = \\alpha\\cos(4t) + \\beta\\sin(4t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t), \\\\ \\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))+7(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ (-4\\alpha +7\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+(7\\alpha +4\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-4\\alpha +7\\beta &=3\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) \\\\ 7\\alpha +4\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha = -\\dfrac{12}{65},$ $\\beta = \\dfrac{21}{65}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\nI_p(t) = -\\dfrac{12}{65}\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{21}{65}\\sin(4t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t) = I_c(t) + I_p(t) = Ae^{-7t} - \\dfrac{12}{65}\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{21}{65}\\sin(4t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity $I_c(t) = Ae^{-7t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n- The function $I_p(t) =  - \\dfrac{12}{65}\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{21}{65}\\sin(4t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to\\infty,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t) \\approx I_p(t) = -\\dfrac{12}{65}\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{21}{65}\\sin(4t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\left(-\\dfrac{12}{65}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{21}{65}\\right)^2} \\approx 0.37\\,\\textrm{A},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places. This amplitude represents the maximum magnitude of the steady-state current flowing through the circuit." "In many cases, the EMF produced by a power source is sinusoidal, not constant. Such a power source is shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/43/4398148f91eda2bae1392f79e868e10c8967ecc23ca9f26b791bc1fbacc45ff9.png)\n\nIn a first-order linear RL circuit driven by a sinusoidal voltage, the solution consists of a transient current that decays over time and a steady-state current that oscillates with the same frequency as the input and is independent of the initial condition.\n\nFor example, let's find the amplitude of the steady-state current in the RL circuit shown below.\n\n![](images/59/59fd8a8db1a227a578670d2457011275446c87ba218686f8d1984f4a73a8cfa6.png)\n\nThe diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $3\\sin(4t)\\,\\textrm{V},$ a resistor of resistance $7\\,\\Omega,$ and an inductor of inductance $1\\,\\textrm{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R = 7\\,\\Omega.$\n\n$$\nV_R = 7I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L = 1\\,\\textrm{H}.$\n\n$$\nV_L = 1\\cdot\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n3\\sin(4t) = 7I + 1\\cdot\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 7I = 3\\sin(4t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution $I_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I_c}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 7I_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\nI_c(t) = Ae^{-7t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $4t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $4t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\nI_p(t) = \\alpha\\cos(4t) + \\beta\\sin(4t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t), \\\\ \\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))+7(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ (-4\\alpha +7\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+(7\\alpha +4\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-4\\alpha +7\\beta &=3\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) \\\\ 7\\alpha +4\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha = -\\dfrac{12}{65},$ $\\beta = \\dfrac{21}{65}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\nI_p(t) = -\\dfrac{12}{65}\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{21}{65}\\sin(4t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t) = I_c(t) + I_p(t) = Ae^{-7t} - \\dfrac{12}{65}\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{21}{65}\\sin(4t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity $I_c(t) = Ae^{-7t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\n- The function $$\n\\displaystyle I_p(t) =  - \\dfrac{12}{65}\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{21}{65}\\sin(4t)\n$$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to\\infty,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t) \\approx I_p(t) = -\\dfrac{12}{65}\\cos(4t) + \\dfrac{21}{65}\\sin(4t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\left(-\\dfrac{12}{65}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{21}{65}\\right)^2} \\approx 0.37\\,\\textrm{A},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places. This amplitude represents the maximum magnitude of the steady-state current flowing through the circuit."] [:db/cas 17592186121001 :tutorial/content "Consider the curve $C$ parametrized by the vector function \n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle 2\\sin t, \\: 2\\cos t, \\: \\sqrt{5} t\\rangle, \\qquad t \\ge 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nFor any $t >0,$ the arc length $s$ of $C$ from $\\mathbf r(0)$ to $\\mathbf r(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}‖{r}^{′}(u)‖\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}‖⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u,\\,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡u,\\,\\sqrt{5}⟩‖\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}\\sqrt{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u)^{2}+(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡u)^{2}+(\\sqrt{5})^{2}}\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}\\sqrt{4({cos}^{2}⁡u+{sin}^{2}⁡u)+5}\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}\\sqrt{9}\\,du \\\\  & =3u{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & =3t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $s= 3t,$ or equivalently $t = \\dfrac{s}{3}.$ \n\nNow, we can define a new vector function $\\mathbf{R}$ as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf R(s)= \\mathbf r\\left(\\dfrac{s}{3}\\right) = \\left\\langle 2\\sin \\left(\\dfrac{s}{3}\\right), \\: 2\\cos \\left(\\dfrac{s}{3}\\right), \\: \\sqrt{5} \\left(\\dfrac{s}{3}\\right) \\right\\rangle .\n$$\n\nThe function $\\mathbf{R}(s)$ gives us the *same* curve $C,$ but this time it is parameterized by the arc length $s.$\n\nIn general, if $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b]$ is a continuously differentiable vector function such that $\\mathbf r'(t )\\neq \\mathbf{0},$ then to parametrize it in terms of arc length we perform the following steps:\n\n1\\. Compute $s = \\displaystyle s(t) = \\int_a^t\\|\\mathbf r'(u)\\| \\, \\mathrm{d}u$\n\n2\\. Write $s = s(t)$ in the form $t=t(s)$\n\n3\\. Substitute $t=t(s)$ back into $\\mathbf r(t)$ to get $\\mathbf R(s)=\\mathbf r(t(s))$\n\n**Note:** One advantage of parametrizing a curve by its arc length is that the tangent vector is always a unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\left\\|\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf R}{\\textrm{d} s}\\right\\| = 1\n$$" "Consider the curve $C$ parametrized by the vector function \n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle 2\\sin t, \\: 2\\cos t, \\: \\sqrt{5} t\\rangle, \\qquad t \\ge 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nFor any $t >0,$ the arc length $s$ of $C$ from $\\mathbf r(0)$ to $\\mathbf r(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}‖{r}^{′}(u)‖\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}‖⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u,\\,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡u,\\,\\sqrt{5}⟩‖\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}\\sqrt{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u)^{2}+(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡u)^{2}+(\\sqrt{5})^{2}}\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}\\sqrt{4({cos}^{2}⁡u+{sin}^{2}⁡u)+5}\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}\\sqrt{9}\\,du \\\\  & =3u{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & =3t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $s= 3t,$ or equivalently $t = \\dfrac{s}{3}.$ \n\nNow, we can define a new vector function $\\mathbf{R}$ as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf R(s)= \\mathbf r\\left(\\dfrac{s}{3}\\right) = \\left\\langle 2\\sin \\left(\\dfrac{s}{3}\\right), \\: 2\\cos \\left(\\dfrac{s}{3}\\right), \\: \\sqrt{5} \\left(\\dfrac{s}{3}\\right) \\right\\rangle .\n$$\n\nThe function $\\mathbf{R}(s)$ gives us the *same* curve $C,$ but this time it is parameterized by the arc length $s.$\n\nIn general, if $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b]$ is a continuously differentiable vector function such that $\\mathbf r'(t )\\neq \\mathbf{0},$ then to parametrize it in terms of arc length we perform the following steps:\n\n1\\. Compute $$\n\\displaystyle s = \\displaystyle s(t) = \\int_a^t\\|\\mathbf r'(u)\\| \\, \\mathrm{d}u\n$$\n\n2\\. Write $s = s(t)$ in the form $t=t(s)$\n\n3\\. Substitute $t=t(s)$ back into $\\mathbf r(t)$ to get $\\mathbf R(s)=\\mathbf r(t(s))$\n\n**Note:** One advantage of parametrizing a curve by its arc length is that the tangent vector is always a unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\left\\|\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf R}{\\textrm{d} s}\\right\\| = 1\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186121015 :tutorial/content "We can use the unit circle to find the sine and cosine of the special *negative* angles. We do this by first finding a coterminal special angle that lies in the range $[0^\\circ , 360^\\circ ),$ and then using $x = \\cos(\\theta)$ and $y = \\sin(\\theta)$ as we did before. \n\n![](images/d9/d93c490adbb5cdbc0e9a09b60eea9b82ab93be8e871e334423abc17ddd62955e.png)\n\nFor example, to find $\\cos(-225^\\circ ),$ first, we find an angle that's coterminal with $-225^\\circ$ that lies in the range $[0,360^\\circ).$\n\n$$\n-225^\\circ + 360^\\circ = 135^\\circ\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\cos\\left(-225^\\circ\\right) = \\cos 135^\\circ.$\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $135^\\circ$ corresponds to the point $\\left( -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}, \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\right).$\n\nSince the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\cos 135^\\circ  = -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, $\\cos \\left(-225^\\circ\\right) =-\\dfrac {\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$" "We can use the unit circle to find the sine and cosine of the special *negative* angles. We do this by first finding a coterminal special angle that lies in the range $[0^\\circ , 360^\\circ ),$ and then using $x = \\cos(\\theta)$ and $y = \\sin(\\theta)$ as we did before. \n\n![](images/d9/d93c490adbb5cdbc0e9a09b60eea9b82ab93be8e871e334423abc17ddd62955e.png)\n\nFor example, to find $\\cos(-225^\\circ ),$ first, we find an angle that's coterminal with $-225^\\circ$ that lies in the range $[0,360^\\circ).$\n\n$$\n-225^\\circ + 360^\\circ = 135^\\circ\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\cos\\left(-225^\\circ\\right) = \\cos 135^\\circ.$\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $135^\\circ$ corresponds to the point $$\n\\displaystyle \\left( -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}, \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\right).\n$$\n\nSince the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\cos 135^\\circ  = -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, $\\cos \\left(-225^\\circ\\right) =-\\dfrac {\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186121016 :tutorial/content "We can also extend our knowledge of special trigonometric ratios to angles greater than $360^\\circ.$ Just like for negative numbers, we find a coterminal special angle that lies in the range $[0^\\circ , 360^\\circ )$, then use $x = \\cos(\\theta)$ and $y = \\sin(\\theta)$ as before.\n\nFor example, to find $\\sin 420^\\circ$, first we find an angle that's coterminal with $420^\\circ$ that lies in the range $[0,360^\\circ).$\n\n$$\n420^\\circ - 360^\\circ = 60^\\circ\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\sin\\left(420^\\circ\\right) = \\sin 60^\\circ.$\n\nLet's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/d9/d93c490adbb5cdbc0e9a09b60eea9b82ab93be8e871e334423abc17ddd62955e.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $60^\\circ$ corresponds to the point $\\left( \\dfrac{1}{2}, \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\right).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\sin 60^\\circ  = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, $\\sin \\left(420^\\circ\\right) =\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$" "We can also extend our knowledge of special trigonometric ratios to angles greater than $360^\\circ.$ Just like for negative numbers, we find a coterminal special angle that lies in the range $[0^\\circ , 360^\\circ )$, then use $x = \\cos(\\theta)$ and $y = \\sin(\\theta)$ as before.\n\nFor example, to find $\\sin 420^\\circ$, first we find an angle that's coterminal with $420^\\circ$ that lies in the range $[0,360^\\circ).$\n\n$$\n420^\\circ - 360^\\circ = 60^\\circ\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\sin\\left(420^\\circ\\right) = \\sin 60^\\circ.$\n\nLet's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/d9/d93c490adbb5cdbc0e9a09b60eea9b82ab93be8e871e334423abc17ddd62955e.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $60^\\circ$ corresponds to the point $$\n\\displaystyle \\left( \\dfrac{1}{2}, \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\right).\n$$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\sin 60^\\circ  = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, $\\sin \\left(420^\\circ\\right) =\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186121026 :tutorial/content "Cramer's rule applies to square systems of linear equations with any number of variables. In this lesson, we will practice applying Cramer's rule to solve $3 \\times 3$ systems of equations.\n\nSuppose we have a square system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y+{a}_{13}z &={b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y+{a}_{23}z &={b}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{31}x+{a}_{32}y+{a}_{33}z &={b}_{3},\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere $D={\\left| \\begin{array}{ccc}{\\color{blue}{a}_{11}} & {\\color{blue}{a}_{12}} & {\\color{blue}{a}_{13}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{a}_{21}} & {\\color{blue}{a}_{22}} & {\\color{blue}{a}_{23}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{a}_{31}} & {\\color{blue}{a}_{32}} & {\\color{blue}{a}_{33}}\\end{array} \\right|} \\neq 0$. \nThen Cramer's rule states that\n\n$$\n\\left< x,y,z \\right> = \\left\\langle  \\dfrac{D_x}{D}, \\dfrac{D_y}{D}, \\dfrac{D_z}{D} \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nis the unique solution of the system, where\n\n$$\nD_x={\\left| \\begin{array}{ccc}{\\color{red}{b}_{1}} & {\\color{blue}{a}_{12}} & {\\color{blue}{a}_{13}} \\\\ {\\color{red}{b}_{2}} & {\\color{blue}{a}_{22}} & {\\color{blue}{a}_{23}} \\\\ {\\color{red}{b}_{3}} & {\\color{blue}{a}_{32}} & {\\color{blue}{a}_{33}}\\end{array} \\right|},\n\t\\qquad\n\tD_y={\\left| \\begin{array}{ccc}{\\color{blue}{a}_{11}} & {\\color{red}{b}_{1}} & {\\color{blue}{a}_{13}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{a}_{21}} & {\\color{red}{b}_{2}} & {\\color{blue}{a}_{23}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{a}_{31}} & {\\color{red}{b}_{3}} & {\\color{blue}{a}_{33}}\\end{array} \\right|},\n\t\\qquad\n\tD_z={\\left| \\begin{array}{ccc}{\\color{blue}{a}_{11}} & {\\color{blue}{a}_{12}} & {\\color{red}{b}_{1}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{a}_{21}} & {\\color{blue}{a}_{22}} & {\\color{red}{b}_{2}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{a}_{31}} & {\\color{blue}{a}_{32}} & {\\color{red}{b}_{3}}\\end{array} \\right|}.\n$$" "Cramer's rule applies to square systems of linear equations with any number of variables. In this lesson, we will practice applying Cramer's rule to solve $3 \\times 3$ systems of equations.\n\nSuppose we have a square system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y+{a}_{13}z &={b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y+{a}_{23}z &={b}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{31}x+{a}_{32}y+{a}_{33}z &={b}_{3},\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle D={\\left| \\begin{array}{ccc}{\\color{blue}{a}_{11}} & {\\color{blue}{a}_{12}} & {\\color{blue}{a}_{13}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{a}_{21}} & {\\color{blue}{a}_{22}} & {\\color{blue}{a}_{23}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{a}_{31}} & {\\color{blue}{a}_{32}} & {\\color{blue}{a}_{33}}\\end{array} \\right|} \\neq 0.\n$$ \nThen Cramer's rule states that\n\n$$\n\\left< x,y,z \\right> = \\left\\langle  \\dfrac{D_x}{D}, \\dfrac{D_y}{D}, \\dfrac{D_z}{D} \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nis the unique solution of the system, where\n\n$$\nD_x={\\left| \\begin{array}{ccc}{\\color{red}{b}_{1}} & {\\color{blue}{a}_{12}} & {\\color{blue}{a}_{13}} \\\\ {\\color{red}{b}_{2}} & {\\color{blue}{a}_{22}} & {\\color{blue}{a}_{23}} \\\\ {\\color{red}{b}_{3}} & {\\color{blue}{a}_{32}} & {\\color{blue}{a}_{33}}\\end{array} \\right|},\n\t\\qquad\n\tD_y={\\left| \\begin{array}{ccc}{\\color{blue}{a}_{11}} & {\\color{red}{b}_{1}} & {\\color{blue}{a}_{13}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{a}_{21}} & {\\color{red}{b}_{2}} & {\\color{blue}{a}_{23}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{a}_{31}} & {\\color{red}{b}_{3}} & {\\color{blue}{a}_{33}}\\end{array} \\right|},\n\t\\qquad\n\tD_z={\\left| \\begin{array}{ccc}{\\color{blue}{a}_{11}} & {\\color{blue}{a}_{12}} & {\\color{red}{b}_{1}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{a}_{21}} & {\\color{blue}{a}_{22}} & {\\color{red}{b}_{2}} \\\\ {\\color{blue}{a}_{31}} & {\\color{blue}{a}_{32}} & {\\color{red}{b}_{3}}\\end{array} \\right|}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186121029 :tutorial/content "Recall that LU factorization involves writing a matrix $A$ as a product of two matrices $L$ and $U,$ where\n\n- $L = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}{\\color{blue}1} & {\\color{red}0} & {\\color{red}0} \\\\ ∗ & {\\color{blue}1} & {\\color{red}0} \\\\ ∗ & ∗ & {\\color{blue}1}\\end{array} \\right]}$ is a *unit lower triangular matrix* (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal), and\n\n- $U = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}∗ & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ {\\color{red}0} & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ {\\color{red}0} & {\\color{red}0} & ∗\\end{array} \\right]}$ is an *upper triangular matrix*.\n\nFor example, suppose we're given the matrix $A = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}1 & - 1 & - 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & - 3 \\\\ - 2 & 2 & 7\\end{array} \\right]}.$ To perform LU factorization, we need to write $A$ as \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}1 & - 1 & - 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & - 3 \\\\ - 2 & 2 & 7\\end{array} \\right]}\n}_{A}\n=\n\\underbrace{\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}{\\color{blue}1} & {\\color{red}0} & {\\color{red}0} \\\\ ∗ & {\\color{blue}1} & {\\color{red}0} \\\\ ∗ & ∗ & {\\color{blue}1}\\end{array} \\right]}\n}_{L}\n\\cdot\n\\underbrace{\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}∗ & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ {\\color{red}0} & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ {\\color{red}0} & {\\color{red}0} & ∗\\end{array} \\right]}\n}_{U}.\n$$\n\nAs usual, to find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we can reduce the matrix $A$ to *echelon form* while simultaneously performing the same elementary row operations on the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 7\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} & ⇔ & \\,\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{{E}_{1}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]=U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since $E_1A=U,$ we can invert $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =\\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{L}\\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Caution:** In order to get a unit lower triangular matrix at the end, we have the following restriction while reducing a matrix $A$ to its *echelon form*:\n\n- we can only add multiples of higher rows to lower rows ($R_j:=R_j+k R_i$ where $i < j$), and\n\n- the other elementary row operations (swapping or multiplying a row by a number) are not allowed." "Recall that LU factorization involves writing a matrix $A$ as a product of two matrices $L$ and $U,$ where\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle L = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}{\\color{blue}1} & {\\color{red}0} & {\\color{red}0} \\\\ ∗ & {\\color{blue}1} & {\\color{red}0} \\\\ ∗ & ∗ & {\\color{blue}1}\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$ is a *unit lower triangular matrix* (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal), and\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle U = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}∗ & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ {\\color{red}0} & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ {\\color{red}0} & {\\color{red}0} & ∗\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$ is an *upper triangular matrix*.\n\nFor example, suppose we're given the matrix $A = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}1 & - 1 & - 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & - 3 \\\\ - 2 & 2 & 7\\end{array} \\right]}.$ To perform LU factorization, we need to write $A$ as \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}1 & - 1 & - 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & - 3 \\\\ - 2 & 2 & 7\\end{array} \\right]}\n}_{A}\n=\n\\underbrace{\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}{\\color{blue}1} & {\\color{red}0} & {\\color{red}0} \\\\ ∗ & {\\color{blue}1} & {\\color{red}0} \\\\ ∗ & ∗ & {\\color{blue}1}\\end{array} \\right]}\n}_{L}\n\\cdot\n\\underbrace{\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}∗ & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ {\\color{red}0} & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ {\\color{red}0} & {\\color{red}0} & ∗\\end{array} \\right]}\n}_{U}.\n$$\n\nAs usual, to find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we can reduce the matrix $A$ to *echelon form* while simultaneously performing the same elementary row operations on the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 7\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} & ⇔ & \\,\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{{E}_{1}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]=U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since $E_1A=U,$ we can invert $E_1$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =\\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{L}\\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Caution:** In order to get a unit lower triangular matrix at the end, we have the following restriction while reducing a matrix $A$ to its *echelon form*:\n\n- we can only add multiples of higher rows to lower rows ($R_j:=R_j+k R_i$ where $i < j$), and\n\n- the other elementary row operations (swapping or multiplying a row by a number) are not allowed."] [:db/cas 17592186124193 :question/problem "The figure below shows the graph of $f(x).$ Find  $\\lim_\\limits{x \\rightarrow \\infty} f(x)$ and $\\lim_\\limits{x \\rightarrow -\\infty} f(x).$ \n\n![](images/c7/c7ee917a93ed51c189979516b9ab0d3232c183921ee277f522f617518ce03fdf.png)" "The figure below shows the graph of $f(x).$ Find  $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{x \\rightarrow \\infty} f(x)\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{x \\rightarrow -\\infty} f(x).\n$$ \n\n![](images/c7/c7ee917a93ed51c189979516b9ab0d3232c183921ee277f522f617518ce03fdf.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186124212 :question/problem "Given the ellipsoid $\\dfrac {x^2} {4} +  \\dfrac {y^2} 9  + z^2 = 1$, find the domain of $z=z(x,y).$" "Given the ellipsoid $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac {x^2} {4} +  \\dfrac {y^2} 9  + z^2 = 1,\n$$ find the domain of $z=z(x,y).$"] [:db/cas 17592186124213 :question/problem "Given the hyperboloid of one sheet  $\\dfrac{(x-3)^2}9 + \\dfrac{(y+4)^2}{16} - \\dfrac{(z-5)^2}{25} = 1,$ find its intercepts with the $x$-axis." "Given the hyperboloid of one sheet  $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{(x-3)^2}9 + \\dfrac{(y+4)^2}{16} - \\dfrac{(z-5)^2}{25} = 1,\n$$ find its intercepts with the $x$-axis."] [:db/cas 17592186124214 :question/problem "Identify the surface $z -1 = \\dfrac{(x-3)^2}{4} - \\dfrac{(y+2)^2}4.$" "Identify the surface $$\n\\displaystyle z -1 = \\dfrac{(x-3)^2}{4} - \\dfrac{(y+2)^2}4.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186124217 :question/worked-solution "The second partial derivative test states that if $\\mathbf{a}\\in\\mathbb{R}^2$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(\\mathbf a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(\\mathbf{a}) > 0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(\\mathbf{a}) <                0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $\\mathbf{a}$\n\n- if $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(\\mathbf{a}) >0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $\\mathbf{a}$\n\n- If $H(\\mathbf{a}) <       0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $\\mathbf{a}$\n\n- If $H(\\mathbf{a}) = 0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we notice that since $𝒇'(x,y) = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}2 - 2 x & 2 + 2 y\\end{array} \\right]},$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} = 2-2x, \n\\qquad \n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} =2+2y.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2-2x)=-2 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2+2y)=2 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2+2y)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,-1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,-1)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the determinant of $\\boldsymbol f''(1,-1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(1,-1) = \\det(\\boldsymbol f''(1,-1)) = -2 \\cdot 2 - 0^2 = -4 < 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(1,-1) = (\\boldsymbol f''(1,-1))_{11} = -2 \\lt 0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $H(1,-1) < 0,$ the point $(1,-1)$ is a saddle point by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "The second partial derivative test states that if $\\mathbf{a}\\in\\mathbb{R}^2$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(\\mathbf a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(\\mathbf{a}) > 0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(\\mathbf{a}) <                0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $\\mathbf{a}$\n\n- if $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(\\mathbf{a}) >0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $\\mathbf{a}$\n\n- If $H(\\mathbf{a}) <       0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $\\mathbf{a}$\n\n- If $H(\\mathbf{a}) = 0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we notice that since $𝒇'(x,y) = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}2 - 2 x & 2 + 2 y\\end{array} \\right]},$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} = 2-2x, \n\\qquad \n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} =2+2y.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2-2x)=-2 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2+2y)=2 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2+2y)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,-1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,-1)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the determinant of $\\boldsymbol f''(1,-1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(1,-1) = \\det(\\boldsymbol f''(1,-1)) = -2 \\cdot 2 - 0^2 = -4 < 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(1,-1) = (\\boldsymbol f''(1,-1))_{11} = -2 \\lt 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $H(1,-1) < 0,$ the point $(1,-1)$ is a saddle point by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186124218 :question/worked-solution "The local extrema of $f$ occurs at its critical points. A point $\\mathbf a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${𝒇}^{\\prime } ( \\mathbf{a} ) = {\\mathbf{0}}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${𝒇}^{\\prime } ( \\mathbf{a} )$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $\\mathbf{a}\\in\\mathbb{R}^2$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(\\mathbf a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(\\mathbf a) > 0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(\\mathbf{a}) < 0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $\\mathbf a$\n\n- if $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(\\mathbf{a}) >0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $\\mathbf a$\n\n- If $H(\\mathbf a) < 0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $\\mathbf a$\n\n- If $H(\\mathbf{a}) = 0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, notice that since ${𝒇}^{\\prime } ( x , y ) = \\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}3 {x}^{2} - 3 & 2 y\\end{array} \\right] ,$ we must have \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} = 3x^2 - 3, \n\\qquad \n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} =2y.\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting ${𝒇}^{\\prime } ( x , y ) = {\\mathbf{0}}^{T} ,$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}3 {x}^{2} - 3 = 0 \\\\ 2 y = 0\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n3x^2 -3= 0\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nx=\\pm 1.\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n2y = 0\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\ny=0.\n$$\n\nHence, $(1,0)$ and $(-1,0)$ are stationary points of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify these stationary points, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rlrl}\\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\partial }^{2} f}{\\partial  {x}^{2}}} & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial  x}} ( 3 {x}^{2} - 3 ) = 6 x \\hspace{2em} & \\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\partial }^{2} f}{\\partial  x \\partial  y}} & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial  x}} ( 2 y ) = 0 \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\partial }^{2} f}{\\partial  y \\partial  x}} & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial  y}} ( 3 {x}^{2} - 3 ) = 0 \\hspace{2em} & \\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\partial }^{2} f}{\\partial  {y}^{2}}} & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial  y}} ( 2 y ) = 2\\end{array}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{cc}{𝒇}^{\\prime \\prime } ( x , y ) &  = \\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}\\frac{{\\partial }^{2} f}{\\partial  {x}^{2}} & \\frac{{\\partial }^{2} f}{\\partial  x \\partial  y} \\\\ \\frac{{\\partial }^{2} f}{\\partial  y \\partial  x} & \\frac{{\\partial }^{2} f}{\\partial  {y}^{2}}\\end{array} \\right] = \\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}6 x & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{array} \\right] .\\end{array}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the second derivative at each of the stationary points and use it to categorize them:\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(1,0),$ we obtain\n$$\n\\begin{array}{c}{𝒇}^{\\prime \\prime } ( 1 , 0 ) = \\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{array} \\right] .\\end{array}\n$$\nComputing the determinant $H(1,0),$ we get\n$$\nH ( 1 , 0 ) = \\det  ( {𝒇}^{\\prime \\prime } ( 1 , 0 ) ) = 6 \\cdot  2 - {0}^{2} = 12 > 0.\n$$\nNow, since $H(1,0) > 0$ and  $\\frac{{\\partial }^{2} f}{\\partial  {x}^{2}} ( 1 , 0 ) = ( {𝒇}^{\\prime \\prime } ( 1 , 0 ) {)}_{11} = 6 > 0 ,$ the function $f$ has a local minimum at $(1,0).$\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(-1,0),$ we obtain\n$$\n\\begin{array}{c}{𝒇}^{\\prime \\prime } ( - 1 , 0 ) = \\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}- 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{array} \\right] .\\end{array}\n$$\nComputing the determinant $H(-1,0),$ we get\n$$\nH ( - 1 , 0 ) = \\det  ( {𝒇}^{\\prime \\prime } ( - 1 , 0 ) ) = - 6 \\cdot  2 - {0}^{2} = - 12 < 0\n$$\nTherefore, $f(-1,0)$ is a saddle point.\n\nIn conclusion, $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(1,0)$ and a saddle point at $(-1,0).$" "The local extrema of $f$ occurs at its critical points. A point $\\mathbf a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${𝒇}^{\\prime } ( \\mathbf{a} ) = {\\mathbf{0}}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${𝒇}^{\\prime } ( \\mathbf{a} )$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $\\mathbf{a}\\in\\mathbb{R}^2$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(\\mathbf a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(\\mathbf a) > 0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(\\mathbf{a}) < 0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $\\mathbf a$\n\n- if $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(\\mathbf{a}) >0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $\\mathbf a$\n\n- If $H(\\mathbf a) < 0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $\\mathbf a$\n\n- If $H(\\mathbf{a}) = 0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, notice that since ${𝒇}^{\\prime } ( x , y ) = \\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}3 {x}^{2} - 3 & 2 y\\end{array} \\right] ,$ we must have \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} = 3x^2 - 3, \n\\qquad \n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} =2y.\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting ${𝒇}^{\\prime } ( x , y ) = {\\mathbf{0}}^{T} ,$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}3 {x}^{2} - 3 = 0 \\\\ 2 y = 0\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n3x^2 -3= 0\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nx=\\pm 1.\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n2y = 0\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\ny=0.\n$$\n\nHence, $(1,0)$ and $(-1,0)$ are stationary points of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify these stationary points, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rlrl}\\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\partial }^{2} f}{\\partial  {x}^{2}}} & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial  x}} ( 3 {x}^{2} - 3 ) = 6 x \\hspace{2em} & \\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\partial }^{2} f}{\\partial  x \\partial  y}} & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial  x}} ( 2 y ) = 0 \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\partial }^{2} f}{\\partial  y \\partial  x}} & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial  y}} ( 3 {x}^{2} - 3 ) = 0 \\hspace{2em} & \\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\partial }^{2} f}{\\partial  {y}^{2}}} & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial  y}} ( 2 y ) = 2\\end{array}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{cc}{𝒇}^{\\prime \\prime } ( x , y ) &  = \\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}\\frac{{\\partial }^{2} f}{\\partial  {x}^{2}} & \\frac{{\\partial }^{2} f}{\\partial  x \\partial  y} \\\\ \\frac{{\\partial }^{2} f}{\\partial  y \\partial  x} & \\frac{{\\partial }^{2} f}{\\partial  {y}^{2}}\\end{array} \\right] = \\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}6 x & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{array} \\right] .\\end{array}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the second derivative at each of the stationary points and use it to categorize them:\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(1,0),$ we obtain\n$$\n\\begin{array}{c}{𝒇}^{\\prime \\prime } ( 1 , 0 ) = \\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{array} \\right] .\\end{array}\n$$\nComputing the determinant $H(1,0),$ we get\n$$\nH ( 1 , 0 ) = \\det  ( {𝒇}^{\\prime \\prime } ( 1 , 0 ) ) = 6 \\cdot  2 - {0}^{2} = 12 > 0.\n$$\nNow, since $H(1,0) > 0$ and  $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{\\partial }^{2} f}{\\partial  {x}^{2}} ( 1 , 0 ) = ( {𝒇}^{\\prime \\prime } ( 1 , 0 ) {)}_{11} = 6 > 0 ,\n$$ the function $f$ has a local minimum at $(1,0).$\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(-1,0),$ we obtain\n$$\n\\begin{array}{c}{𝒇}^{\\prime \\prime } ( - 1 , 0 ) = \\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}- 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{array} \\right] .\\end{array}\n$$\nComputing the determinant $H(-1,0),$ we get\n$$\nH ( - 1 , 0 ) = \\det  ( {𝒇}^{\\prime \\prime } ( - 1 , 0 ) ) = - 6 \\cdot  2 - {0}^{2} = - 12 < 0\n$$\nTherefore, $f(-1,0)$ is a saddle point.\n\nIn conclusion, $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(1,0)$ and a saddle point at $(-1,0).$"] [:db/cas 17592186124238 :question/problem "The figure below shows the graphs of $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$. Evaluate $\\lim_\\limits{x \\rightarrow \\,-2} \\Bigl(3f(x) - g(x)\\Bigr)$.\n\n![](images/43/439ebf5a1e5812c6fb27da2dd4b3015ea757133343b86695ebad44db6eaeae7c.png)" "The figure below shows the graphs of $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$. Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{x \\rightarrow \\,-2} \\Bigl(3f(x) - g(x)\\Bigr).\n$$\n\n![](images/43/439ebf5a1e5812c6fb27da2dd4b3015ea757133343b86695ebad44db6eaeae7c.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186124243 :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=1.$ Given that the point $P(0,1,1)$ lies on $C,$ and that $f_x =  \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2} \\pi}{4} \\sin{\\left(2 x + \\dfrac{\\pi y}{4} \\right)},$ find a tangent vector to $C$ at $P.$" "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=1.$ Given that the point $P(0,1,1)$ lies on $C,$ and that $$\n\\displaystyle f_x =  \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2} \\pi}{4} \\sin{\\left(2 x + \\dfrac{\\pi y}{4} \\right)},\n$$ find a tangent vector to $C$ at $P.$"] [:db/cas 17592186124244 :question/problem "Find $\\dfrac {\\partial^2 f }{\\partial x\\partial y}$ given that $\\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial y} =  \\dfrac {2y} {x + y^2}.$" "Find $\\dfrac {\\partial^2 f }{\\partial x\\partial y}$ given that $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial y} =  \\dfrac {2y} {x + y^2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186124256 :question/problem "Find  $\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,2)}} \\dfrac{xy^3}{x+y}.$" "Find  $$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,2)}} \\dfrac{xy^3}{x+y}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186124257 :question/problem "Find  $\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1)}} \\dfrac{x^2y+xy^2}{x^2-y^2}.$" "Find  $$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1)}} \\dfrac{x^2y+xy^2}{x^2-y^2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186124261 :question/problem "Find  $\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}} \\dfrac{x(x + 2y)}{y^2}.$" "Find  $$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}} \\dfrac{x(x + 2y)}{y^2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186124264 :question/worked-solution "At a given point, a function $g(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nIn our case, we compute $\\nabla g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $\\left(\\dfrac 12, \\dfrac 32 \\right)\\!\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}})),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{3}}{2\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}},\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}}\\cdot \\frac{3x{y}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2x},\\,\\frac{3}{2y}⟩ \\\\ ∇g(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2}) & =⟨\\frac{1}{2\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)},\\frac{3}{2\\cdot \\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $\\left(\\dfrac 12, \\dfrac 32 \\right)$ in the direction of $\\nabla g\\left(\\dfrac 12, \\dfrac 32 \\right)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖∇g(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2})‖ & =‖⟨1,1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "At a given point, a function $g(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nIn our case, we compute $\\nabla g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $$\n\\displaystyle \\left(\\dfrac 12, \\dfrac 32 \\right)\\!\\mathbin{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}})),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{3}}{2\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}},\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}}\\cdot \\frac{3x{y}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{x{y}^{3}}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2x},\\,\\frac{3}{2y}⟩ \\\\ ∇g(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2}) & =⟨\\frac{1}{2\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)},\\frac{3}{2\\cdot \\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the rate of change of $g(x,y)$ at the point $\\left(\\dfrac 12, \\dfrac 32 \\right)$ in the direction of $$\n\\displaystyle \\nabla g\\left(\\dfrac 12, \\dfrac 32 \\right)\n$$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖∇g(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2})‖ & =‖⟨1,1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186124266 :question/worked-solution "To find the equation of the normal line, we first find the equation of a normal vector, and then write the equation of the line that is parallel to the normal vector.\n\nTo obtain a normal vector, we write our curve as a level curve and then compute the gradient. Bringing all the terms of our equation to one side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{x^2}9 + \\dfrac {y^2}{16} - 1,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$   \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(0,4)$ can be found by computing $\\nabla f(0,4).$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-1)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-1)j \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{9}\\text{ }i+\\frac{y}{8}\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(0,4) & =\\frac{2(0)}{9}\\text{ }i+\\frac{(4)}{8}\\text{ }j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\text{ }j \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,\\frac{1}{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\nabla f(0,4)= \\left\\langle 0, \\:\\dfrac12 \\right\\rangle$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(0,4).$\n\nNow, remember that the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\mathbf{v} = \\langle v_x, v_y \\rangle$ and passes through the point $(x_0,y_0)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{x-{x}_{0}}=\\frac{{v}_{y}}{{v}_{x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nor, equivalently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{y}(x-{x}_{0}) & ={v}_{x}(y-{y}_{0}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\nabla f(0,4)= \\left\\langle 0, \\:\\dfrac12 \\right\\rangle,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(x-0) & =0(y-4) \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the equation of the normal line to the ellipse $\\dfrac{x^2}9 + \\dfrac {y^2}{16}=1$ at the point $(0,4)$ is $x=0.$" "To find the equation of the normal line, we first find the equation of a normal vector, and then write the equation of the line that is parallel to the normal vector.\n\nTo obtain a normal vector, we write our curve as a level curve and then compute the gradient. Bringing all the terms of our equation to one side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $$\n\\displaystyle f(x,y) = \\dfrac{x^2}9 + \\dfrac {y^2}{16} - 1,\n$$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$   \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(0,4)$ can be found by computing $\\nabla f(0,4).$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-1)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-1)j \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{9}\\text{ }i+\\frac{y}{8}\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(0,4) & =\\frac{2(0)}{9}\\text{ }i+\\frac{(4)}{8}\\text{ }j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\text{ }j \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,\\frac{1}{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\nabla f(0,4)= \\left\\langle 0, \\:\\dfrac12 \\right\\rangle$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(0,4).$\n\nNow, remember that the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\mathbf{v} = \\langle v_x, v_y \\rangle$ and passes through the point $(x_0,y_0)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{x-{x}_{0}}=\\frac{{v}_{y}}{{v}_{x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nor, equivalently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{y}(x-{x}_{0}) & ={v}_{x}(y-{y}_{0}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\nabla f(0,4)= \\left\\langle 0, \\:\\dfrac12 \\right\\rangle,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(x-0) & =0(y-4) \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the equation of the normal line to the ellipse $\\dfrac{x^2}9 + \\dfrac {y^2}{16}=1$ at the point $(0,4)$ is $x=0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186124268 :question/worked-solution "To find the equation of the normal line, we first find the equation of a normal vector, and then write the equation of the line that is parallel to the normal vector.\n\nTo obtain a normal vector, we write our curve as a level curve and then compute the gradient. Bringing all the terms of our equation to one side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-xy) \\\\ 0 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-xy)-z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z) = \\ln (x^2 - xy) - z,$ our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $\\left(2, 1, \\ln 2 \\right)$ can be found by computing $\\nabla f(2,1,\\ln 2){:}$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-xy)-z)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-xy)-z)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-xy)-z)k \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{2x-y}{{x}^{2}-xy}\\right)\\text{ }i+\\left(\\frac{1}{y-x}\\right)\\text{ }j-\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(2,1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =\\left(\\frac{2\\cdot 2-1}{{2}^{2}-2\\cdot 1}\\right)\\text{ }i+\\left(\\frac{1}{1-2}\\right)\\text{ }j-\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}i-\\,j-k \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{3}{2},-1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, remember that the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\mathbf v = \\langle v_x, v_y, v_z \\rangle$ and passes through the point $(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\nabla f(2,1,\\ln 2)= \\left\\langle  \\dfrac {3} {2}, -1, -1\\right  \\rangle,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-2}{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}=\\frac{y-1}{-1}=\\frac{z-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{-1} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{3}=\\frac{y-1}{-2}=\\frac{z-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To find the equation of the normal line, we first find the equation of a normal vector, and then write the equation of the line that is parallel to the normal vector.\n\nTo obtain a normal vector, we write our curve as a level curve and then compute the gradient. Bringing all the terms of our equation to one side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-xy) \\\\ 0 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-xy)-z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z) = \\ln (x^2 - xy) - z,$ our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $\\left(2, 1, \\ln 2 \\right)$ can be found by computing $\\nabla f(2,1,\\ln 2){:}$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-xy)-z)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-xy)-z)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-xy)-z)k \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{2x-y}{{x}^{2}-xy}\\right)\\text{ }i+\\left(\\frac{1}{y-x}\\right)\\text{ }j-\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(2,1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =\\left(\\frac{2\\cdot 2-1}{{2}^{2}-2\\cdot 1}\\right)\\text{ }i+\\left(\\frac{1}{1-2}\\right)\\text{ }j-\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}i-\\,j-k \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{3}{2},-1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, remember that the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\mathbf v = \\langle v_x, v_y, v_z \\rangle$ and passes through the point $(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $$\n\\displaystyle \\nabla f(2,1,\\ln 2)= \\left\\langle  \\dfrac {3} {2}, -1, -1\\right  \\rangle,\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-2}{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}=\\frac{y-1}{-1}=\\frac{z-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{-1} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{3}=\\frac{y-1}{-2}=\\frac{z-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186124271 :question/problem "The figure below shows the graph of $f(x).$ Find the limit $\\lim_\\limits{x \\rightarrow \\infty} f(x).$ \n\n![](images/49/4982eb6e9020e5f6235049fd36a030a69d7b74a0e3ea60323ac8887f4c9bca79.png)" "The figure below shows the graph of $f(x).$ Find the limit $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{x \\rightarrow \\infty} f(x).\n$$ \n\n![](images/49/4982eb6e9020e5f6235049fd36a030a69d7b74a0e3ea60323ac8887f4c9bca79.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186124272 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_\\limits{x \\rightarrow \\,1} \\left(6x^2+x\\right)$." "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{x \\rightarrow \\,1} \\left(6x^2+x\\right).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186124273 :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_\\limits {x\\to (\\pi/4)} 2\\sec \\left(2x\\right)$." "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits {x\\to (\\pi/4)} 2\\sec \\left(2x\\right).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186124274 :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_\\limits {x \\to \\pi} \\csc x$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits {x \\to \\pi} \\csc x\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186124275 :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_\\limits {x \\to \\pi/3} \\cot \\left(3x\\right)$." "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits {x \\to \\pi/3} \\cot \\left(3x\\right).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186124286 :question/problem "Compute $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to (-1)} \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^3} \\, .$" "Compute $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{x \\to (-1)} \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^3} \\, .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186124289 :question/problem "Calculate  $\\lim_\\limits {x\\rightarrow -1} \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^4} .$" "Calculate  $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits {x\\rightarrow -1} \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^4} .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186124302 :question/problem "Consider the vector space $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ of all $2 \\times 2$ matrices over $\\mathbb{R}.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\bigg\\{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}0 & - 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{array} \\right]}, {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{array} \\right]} \\bigg\\}$ is a linearly independent set in $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$\n\nII. $\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $\\bigg\\{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}0 & - 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{array} \\right]}, {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{array} \\right]} \\bigg\\}$\n\nIII. $\\bigg\\{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}0 & - 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{array} \\right]}, {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{array} \\right]} \\bigg\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$" "Consider the vector space $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ of all $2 \\times 2$ matrices over $\\mathbb{R}.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle \\bigg\\{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}0 & - 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{array} \\right]}, {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{array} \\right]} \\bigg\\}\n$$ is a linearly independent set in $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$\n\nII. $\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $$\n\\displaystyle \\bigg\\{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}0 & - 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{array} \\right]}, {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{array} \\right]} \\bigg\\}\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle \\bigg\\{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}0 & - 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{array} \\right]}, {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{array} \\right]} \\bigg\\}\n$$ is a basis of $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$"] [:db/cas 17592186124307 :question/problem "Let $\\mathcal{B}=\\bigg\\{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ 7\\end{array} \\right]}, {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 4 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]} \\bigg\\}$ and $\\mathcal{C}=\\bigg\\{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 3\\end{array} \\right]}, {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 2 \\\\ - 5\\end{array} \\right]} \\bigg\\}$ be bases of $\\mathbb{R}^2.$ Find the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C}.$" "Let $$\n\\displaystyle \\mathcal{B}=\\bigg\\{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ 7\\end{array} \\right]}, {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 4 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]} \\bigg\\}\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\mathcal{C}=\\bigg\\{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 3\\end{array} \\right]}, {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 2 \\\\ - 5\\end{array} \\right]} \\bigg\\}\n$$ be bases of $\\mathbb{R}^2.$ Find the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186124311 :question/problem "Given the ellipsoid $\\dfrac {(x-2)^2} {2} +  {y^2} + \\dfrac{(z+1)^2}{2} =1,$ find its intercepts with the $x$-axis." "Given the ellipsoid $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac {(x-2)^2} {2} +  {y^2} + \\dfrac{(z+1)^2}{2} =1,\n$$ find its intercepts with the $x$-axis."] [:db/cas 17592186124319 :question/problem "Given $A={\\left[ \\begin{array}{cccc}2 & 1 & 3 & - 2 \\\\ 0 & - 3 & - 2 & - 1 \\\\ - 6 & 0 & - 7 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 6 & 3\\end{array} \\right]},\\;$ what is $\\textrm{nullity}(A)?$" "Given $$\n\\displaystyle A={\\left[ \\begin{array}{cccc}2 & 1 & 3 & - 2 \\\\ 0 & - 3 & - 2 & - 1 \\\\ - 6 & 0 & - 7 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 6 & 3\\end{array} \\right]},\\;\n$$ what is $\\textrm{nullity}(A)?$"] [:db/cas 17592186124325 :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}:\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x \\\\ y\\end{array} \\right]}\\longrightarrow\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 x + 4 y \\\\ 3 x + 6 y\\end{array} \\right]}.$ Find $\\textrm{Ker}(\\mathbf{T}).$" "Consider the linear transformation $$\n\\displaystyle \\mathbf{T}:\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x \\\\ y\\end{array} \\right]}\\longrightarrow\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 x + 4 y \\\\ 3 x + 6 y\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$ Find $\\textrm{Ker}(\\mathbf{T}).$"] [:db/cas 17592186124326 :question/problem "Given the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}:\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{array} \\right]}\\longrightarrow\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 x + 4 y \\\\ 4 x + 10 y - z \\\\ z - 6 x - 12 y\\end{array} \\right]},$ find $\\,\\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T}).$" "Given the linear transformation $$\n\\displaystyle \\mathbf{T}:\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{array} \\right]}\\longrightarrow\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 x + 4 y \\\\ 4 x + 10 y - z \\\\ z - 6 x - 12 y\\end{array} \\right]},\n$$ find $\\,\\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T}).$"] [:db/cas 17592186124327 :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}:\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{array} \\right]}\n\\longrightarrow \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}3 x + 12 y - 3 z \\\\ - 2 x + 4 y - 2 z \\\\ 6 x - 3 y + 3 z\\end{array} \\right]}$ and the vector $\\,\\mathbf{u}={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}k \\\\ 2 \\\\ 6\\end{array} \\right]}.$ Find the value of $\\,k\\,$ such that $\\,\\mathbf{u}\\in\\textrm{Ker}(\\mathbf{T}).$" "Consider the linear transformation $$\n\\displaystyle \\mathbf{T}:\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{array} \\right]}\n\\longrightarrow \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}3 x + 12 y - 3 z \\\\ - 2 x + 4 y - 2 z \\\\ 6 x - 3 y + 3 z\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$ and the vector $\\,\\mathbf{u}={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}k \\\\ 2 \\\\ 6\\end{array} \\right]}.$ Find the value of $\\,k\\,$ such that $\\,\\mathbf{u}\\in\\textrm{Ker}(\\mathbf{T}).$"] [:db/cas 17592186124329 :question/problem "If $T={\\left[ \\begin{array}{cccc}2 & - 3 & - 1 & - 2 \\\\ - 6 & 5 & - 1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array} \\right]}$ is the standard matrix of the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ then what is $\\text{rank}(\\mathbf{T})?$" "If $$\n\\displaystyle T={\\left[ \\begin{array}{cccc}2 & - 3 & - 1 & - 2 \\\\ - 6 & 5 & - 1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$ is the standard matrix of the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ then what is $\\text{rank}(\\mathbf{T})?$"] [:db/cas 17592186124330 :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathbf{T}$ is a linear transformation from $\\mathbb{R}^3$ into $\\mathbb{R}^3,$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $\\mathbf{T}$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad\n\\left\\{\\;\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{Im} ( \\mathbf{T} ) = {\\mathbb{R}}^{3} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{rank} ( \\mathbf{T} ) = 3 \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{Ker} ( \\mathbf{T} ) = \\{  \\mathbf{0} \\}  \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{nullity} ( \\mathbf{T} ) = 0\\end{array}} \\right.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $T$ is invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\textrm{Ker}(\\mathbf{T}) = \\{\\mathbf{0}\\}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $T$ is invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T}) = 0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $T$ is invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T}) = 3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "Since $\\mathbf{T}$ is a linear transformation from $\\mathbb{R}^3$ into $\\mathbb{R}^3,$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $\\mathbf{T}$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $$\n\\displaystyle \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad\n\\left\\{\\;\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{Im} ( \\mathbf{T} ) = {\\mathbb{R}}^{3} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{rank} ( \\mathbf{T} ) = 3 \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{Ker} ( \\mathbf{T} ) = \\{  \\mathbf{0} \\}  \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{nullity} ( \\mathbf{T} ) = 0\\end{array}} \\right.\n$$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $T$ is invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\textrm{Ker}(\\mathbf{T}) = \\{\\mathbf{0}\\}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $T$ is invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\textrm{nullity}(\\mathbf{T}) = 0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $T$ is invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T}) = 3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186124331 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix $\\, A-\\lambda I \\mathbin{:}$\n﻿﻿﻿$A - \\lambda I = \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}- 9 - \\lambda  & 4 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 9 - \\lambda  & - 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 - \\lambda \\end{array} \\right]}$\n\nThe characteristic equation is $\\det(A-\\lambda I) = 0.$ \n\nSince the determinant of a triangular (or diagonal) matrix is given by the product of the entries on the main diagonal, we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-9-\\lambda  & 4 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 9-\\lambda  & -7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-9-\\lambda )(9-\\lambda )(6-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +9)(\\lambda -9)(\\lambda -6) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda_1=-9,$ $\\lambda_2=9,$ and $\\lambda_3=6.$ \n\n**Note:** The eigenvalues of a triangular (or diagonal) matrix are always the entries on the main diagonal." "First, we find the matrix $\\, A-\\lambda I \\mathbin{:}$\n﻿﻿﻿$$\n\\displaystyle A - \\lambda I = \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}- 9 - \\lambda  & 4 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 9 - \\lambda  & - 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 - \\lambda \\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is $\\det(A-\\lambda I) = 0.$ \n\nSince the determinant of a triangular (or diagonal) matrix is given by the product of the entries on the main diagonal, we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-9-\\lambda  & 4 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 9-\\lambda  & -7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-9-\\lambda )(9-\\lambda )(6-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +9)(\\lambda -9)(\\lambda -6) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda_1=-9,$ $\\lambda_2=9,$ and $\\lambda_3=6.$ \n\n**Note:** The eigenvalues of a triangular (or diagonal) matrix are always the entries on the main diagonal."] [:db/cas 17592186124332 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix $A-\\lambda I{:}$\n﻿﻿﻿$A - \\lambda I = \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}1 - \\lambda  & - 1 & - 2 \\\\ - 1 & 1 - \\lambda  & - 2 \\\\ - 2 & - 2 & - 7 - \\lambda \\end{array} \\right]}$\n\nThe characteristic equation is given by $\\det(A-\\lambda I)=0.$ \n\nExpanding the determinant across the $1$st row, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{cc}\\det  ( A - \\lambda  I ) &  = 0 \\\\ ∣ \\begin{array}{ccc}1 - \\lambda  & - 1 & - 2 \\\\ - 1 & 1 - \\lambda  & - 2 \\\\ - 2 & - 2 & - 7 - \\lambda \\end{array} ∣ &  = 0 \\\\ ( 1 - \\lambda  ) ∣ \\begin{array}{cc}1 - \\lambda  & - 2 \\\\ - 2 & - 7 - \\lambda \\end{array} ∣ - ( - 1 ) ∣ \\begin{array}{cc}- 1 & - 2 \\\\ - 2 & - 7 - \\lambda \\end{array} ∣ + ( - 2 ) ∣ \\begin{array}{cc}- 1 & 1 - \\lambda  \\\\ - 2 & - 2\\end{array} ∣ &  = 0 \\\\ ( - {\\lambda }^{3} - 5 {\\lambda }^{2} + 17 \\lambda  - 11 ) + ( 3 + \\lambda  ) - 2 ( 4 - 2 \\lambda  ) &  = 0 \\\\ - {\\lambda }^{3} - 5 {\\lambda }^{2} + 22 \\lambda  - 16 &  = 0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{3} + 5 {\\lambda }^{2} - 22 \\lambda  + 16 &  = 0\\end{array}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to solve this equation. We find the first root by testing some values. According to the rational zeros theorem, a list of good first guesses consists of all divisors of $16\\mathbin{:}$ \n$$\n\\pm1, \\pm2, \\pm4, \\pm8, \\pm 16\n$$\n\nLet's pick $\\lambda=1.$ Substituting to the characteristic equation gives\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle ( 1 {)}^{3} + 5 ( 1 {)}^{2} - 22 ( 1 ) + 16 & \\displaystyle  = 0.\\end{array}\n$$\nSo, $\\lambda=1$ is a root of the equation. This means we can factor $(\\lambda-1)$ out of $\\lambda^3 +5 \\lambda^2 - 22 \\lambda + 16$ using synthetic division:\n\n| $1$ | $1$ | $5$ | $-22$ | $16$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$ | $1$ | $6$ | $-16$ |\n|  | $1$ | $6$ | $-16$ | $0$ |\n\nSo, we obtain the following factorization:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(λ - 1)(λ^{2} + 6λ - 16) &= 0 \\\\\n(λ - 1)(λ - 2)(λ + 8) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda_1=1,$ $\\lambda_2=2,$ and $\\,\\lambda_3=-8.$" "First, we find the matrix $A-\\lambda I{:}$\n﻿﻿﻿$$\n\\displaystyle A - \\lambda I = \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}1 - \\lambda  & - 1 & - 2 \\\\ - 1 & 1 - \\lambda  & - 2 \\\\ - 2 & - 2 & - 7 - \\lambda \\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is given by $\\det(A-\\lambda I)=0.$ \n\nExpanding the determinant across the $1$st row, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{cc}\\det  ( A - \\lambda  I ) &  = 0 \\\\ ∣ \\begin{array}{ccc}1 - \\lambda  & - 1 & - 2 \\\\ - 1 & 1 - \\lambda  & - 2 \\\\ - 2 & - 2 & - 7 - \\lambda \\end{array} ∣ &  = 0 \\\\ ( 1 - \\lambda  ) ∣ \\begin{array}{cc}1 - \\lambda  & - 2 \\\\ - 2 & - 7 - \\lambda \\end{array} ∣ - ( - 1 ) ∣ \\begin{array}{cc}- 1 & - 2 \\\\ - 2 & - 7 - \\lambda \\end{array} ∣ + ( - 2 ) ∣ \\begin{array}{cc}- 1 & 1 - \\lambda  \\\\ - 2 & - 2\\end{array} ∣ &  = 0 \\\\ ( - {\\lambda }^{3} - 5 {\\lambda }^{2} + 17 \\lambda  - 11 ) + ( 3 + \\lambda  ) - 2 ( 4 - 2 \\lambda  ) &  = 0 \\\\ - {\\lambda }^{3} - 5 {\\lambda }^{2} + 22 \\lambda  - 16 &  = 0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{3} + 5 {\\lambda }^{2} - 22 \\lambda  + 16 &  = 0\\end{array}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to solve this equation. We find the first root by testing some values. According to the rational zeros theorem, a list of good first guesses consists of all divisors of $16\\mathbin{:}$ \n$$\n\\pm1, \\pm2, \\pm4, \\pm8, \\pm 16\n$$\n\nLet's pick $\\lambda=1.$ Substituting to the characteristic equation gives\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle ( 1 {)}^{3} + 5 ( 1 {)}^{2} - 22 ( 1 ) + 16 & \\displaystyle  = 0.\\end{array}\n$$\nSo, $\\lambda=1$ is a root of the equation. This means we can factor $(\\lambda-1)$ out of $\\lambda^3 +5 \\lambda^2 - 22 \\lambda + 16$ using synthetic division:\n\n| $1$ | $1$ | $5$ | $-22$ | $16$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$ | $1$ | $6$ | $-16$ |\n|  | $1$ | $6$ | $-16$ | $0$ |\n\nSo, we obtain the following factorization:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(λ - 1)(λ^{2} + 6λ - 16) &= 0 \\\\\n(λ - 1)(λ - 2)(λ + 8) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda_1=1,$ $\\lambda_2=2,$ and $\\,\\lambda_3=-8.$"] [:db/cas 17592186124338 :question/worked-solution "Given a $4 \\times 4$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\qquad \\Leftrightarrow \\qquad\n\\left\\{\\;\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{Col} ( M ) = {\\mathbb{R}}^{4} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{dim(Col} ( M ) ) = 4 = \\text{rank} ( M ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{Null} ( M ) = \\{  \\mathbf{0} \\}  \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{dim(Null} ( M ) ) = 0 = \\text{nullity} ( M )\\end{array}} \\right.$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $4$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(M) + \\textrm{nullity}(M) = 4.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(B+4I) & =4-\\text{rank}(B+4I) \\\\  & =4-3 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, $\\textrm{Null}(B+4I) \\ne \\{\\mathbf{0} \\}.$\n\n- Statements II is true. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\textrm{nullity}(B+4I) \\neq 0,$ we obtain that $B +4I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that if $M=B +4 I$ is singular, then $(B +4 I)\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}$ has nonzero solutions which will be eigenvectors of $B.$ As a result, $\\lambda=-4$ is an eigenvalue of $B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "Given a $4 \\times 4$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $$\n\\displaystyle \\qquad \\Leftrightarrow \\qquad\n\\left\\{\\;\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{Col} ( M ) = {\\mathbb{R}}^{4} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{dim(Col} ( M ) ) = 4 = \\text{rank} ( M ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{Null} ( M ) = \\{  \\mathbf{0} \\}  \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{dim(Null} ( M ) ) = 0 = \\text{nullity} ( M )\\end{array}} \\right.\n$$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $4$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(M) + \\textrm{nullity}(M) = 4.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(B+4I) & =4-\\text{rank}(B+4I) \\\\  & =4-3 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, $\\textrm{Null}(B+4I) \\ne \\{\\mathbf{0} \\}.$\n\n- Statements II is true. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\textrm{nullity}(B+4I) \\neq 0,$ we obtain that $B +4I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that if $M=B +4 I$ is singular, then $(B +4 I)\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}$ has nonzero solutions which will be eigenvectors of $B.$ As a result, $\\lambda=-4$ is an eigenvalue of $B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186124343 :question/worked-solution "From the diagonal matrix $D$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are  \n\n$\\qquad$ $\\lambda_1=-2, \\quad$ $\\lambda_2=-1, \\quad$ $\\lambda_3=2.$ \n\nSo, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_1=-2.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda_1=-2.$ Computing $A-(-2)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & -4 & 6 \\\\ -9 & -7 & 9 \\\\ -16 & -10 & 14\\end{bmatrix}-(-2)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -4 & 6 \\\\ -9 & -5 & 9 \\\\ -16 & -10 & 16\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ that we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -4 & 6 & 0 \\\\ -9 & -5 & 9 & 0 \\\\ -16 & -10 & 16 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\left(-\\frac{8}{3}\\right){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -4 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{3} & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\left(-\\frac{2}{3}\\right){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -4 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, $x_3$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain $x_2=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get $x_1=x_3.$\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{x}_{3} \\\\ 0 \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{array} \\right]},\n\t\\quad x_3 \\in \\mathbb{R}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting $x_3=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}_1 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $P = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}\\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{1} & 0 & 1 \\\\ \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{0} & 3 & 2 \\\\ \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{1} & 2 & 3\\end{array} \\right]}.$" "From the diagonal matrix $D$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are  \n\n$\\qquad$ $\\lambda_1=-2, \\quad$ $\\lambda_2=-1, \\quad$ $\\lambda_3=2.$ \n\nSo, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_1=-2.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda_1=-2.$ Computing $A-(-2)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & -4 & 6 \\\\ -9 & -7 & 9 \\\\ -16 & -10 & 14\\end{bmatrix}-(-2)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -4 & 6 \\\\ -9 & -5 & 9 \\\\ -16 & -10 & 16\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ that we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -4 & 6 & 0 \\\\ -9 & -5 & 9 & 0 \\\\ -16 & -10 & 16 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\left(-\\frac{8}{3}\\right){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -4 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{3} & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\left(-\\frac{2}{3}\\right){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -4 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, $x_3$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain $x_2=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get $x_1=x_3.$\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{x}_{3} \\\\ 0 \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{array} \\right]},\n\t\\quad x_3 \\in \\mathbb{R}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting $x_3=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}_1 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $$\n\\displaystyle P = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{ccc}\\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{1} & 0 & 1 \\\\ \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{0} & 3 & 2 \\\\ \\bbox[2pt,lightgray]{1} & 2 & 3\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186124356 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{T}: {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{array} \\right]} \\longrightarrow {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x + y \\\\ - x + y \\\\ 2 y\\end{array} \\right]}$ be a linear transformation. Determine the value of $k$ for which the vector $\\mathbf{v}={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}4 \\\\ k \\\\ 8\\end{array} \\right]}$ belongs to $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T}).$" "Let $$\n\\displaystyle \\mathbf{T}: {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{array} \\right]} \\longrightarrow {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x + y \\\\ - x + y \\\\ 2 y\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$ be a linear transformation. Determine the value of $k$ for which the vector $\\mathbf{v}={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}4 \\\\ k \\\\ 8\\end{array} \\right]}$ belongs to $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T}).$"] [:db/cas 17592186124367 :question/problem "Find  $\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,1)}} \\dfrac{x^2-4y}{x-2\\sqrt{y}}.$" "Find  $$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,1)}} \\dfrac{x^2-4y}{x-2\\sqrt{y}}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186124380 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix $\\, P-\\lambda I \\mathbin{:}$\n﻿﻿﻿$P - \\lambda I = \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cccc}3 - \\lambda  & 0 & - 8 & - 2 \\\\ 0 & - 1 - \\lambda  & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 - \\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & - 3 & 1 - \\lambda \\end{array} \\right]}$\n\nThe characteristic equation is $\\det(P-\\lambda I) = 0.$ \n\nNotice that we can expand the $4 \\times 4$ determinant across the $1$st column, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(P-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}3-\\lambda  & 0 & -8 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1-\\lambda  & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -3 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 4-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 7 & -3 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, expanding the $3 \\times 3$ determinant across the $2$nd row, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3-\\lambda )\\cdot (4-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ 7 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )(4-\\lambda )((-1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-35) & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )(4-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-36) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -3)(\\lambda -4)(\\lambda -6)(\\lambda +6) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues of $P$ are $\\lambda=3,$ $\\lambda=4,$ $\\lambda=6,$ and $\\lambda=-6$ (all with algebraic multiplicity $1$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "First, we find the matrix $\\, P-\\lambda I \\mathbin{:}$\n﻿﻿﻿$$\n\\displaystyle P - \\lambda I = \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cccc}3 - \\lambda  & 0 & - 8 & - 2 \\\\ 0 & - 1 - \\lambda  & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 - \\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & - 3 & 1 - \\lambda \\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is $\\det(P-\\lambda I) = 0.$ \n\nNotice that we can expand the $4 \\times 4$ determinant across the $1$st column, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(P-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}3-\\lambda  & 0 & -8 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1-\\lambda  & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -3 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 4-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 7 & -3 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, expanding the $3 \\times 3$ determinant across the $2$nd row, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3-\\lambda )\\cdot (4-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ 7 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )(4-\\lambda )((-1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-35) & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )(4-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-36) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -3)(\\lambda -4)(\\lambda -6)(\\lambda +6) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues of $P$ are $\\lambda=3,$ $\\lambda=4,$ $\\lambda=6,$ and $\\lambda=-6$ (all with algebraic multiplicity $1$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186124386 :question/problem "Compute $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} \\dfrac{1}{4x^4}.$" "Compute $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} \\dfrac{1}{4x^4}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186124387 :question/problem "Compute $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to -\\infty} \\dfrac{1}{(1-x)^4} \\, .$" "Compute $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{x \\to -\\infty} \\dfrac{1}{(1-x)^4} \\, .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186124388 :question/worked-solution "The side-side-side (SSS) similarity criterion states the following:\n\n> Two triangles are similar if the ratios of the lengths of their corresponding sides are equal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given triangles.\n\n- $\\mathcal{P}$ is similar to $\\mathcal{T}$ by SSS since the ratios of the lengths of the corresponding sides are equal:\n$\\dfrac{16}{8}=\\dfrac{12}{6} =\\dfrac{8}{4}= 2$\n\n- $\\mathcal{Q}$ is *not* similar to $\\mathcal{T}$ by SSS since the ratios of the lengths of the corresponding sides are not equal:\n$\\dfrac{12}{8}\\neq\\dfrac{10}{6}\\neq\\dfrac{8}{4}$.\n- $\\mathcal{R}$ is *not* simlar to $\\mathcal{T}$ by SSS since the ratios of the lengths of the corresponding sides are not equal:\n$\\dfrac{12}{8}\\neq\\dfrac{10}{6}\\neq\\dfrac{9}{4}$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$\\mathcal{P}$ only.\"" "The side-side-side (SSS) similarity criterion states the following:\n\n> Two triangles are similar if the ratios of the lengths of their corresponding sides are equal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given triangles.\n\n- $\\mathcal{P}$ is similar to $\\mathcal{T}$ by SSS since the ratios of the lengths of the corresponding sides are equal:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{16}{8}=\\dfrac{12}{6} =\\dfrac{8}{4}= 2\n$$\n\n- $\\mathcal{Q}$ is *not* similar to $\\mathcal{T}$ by SSS since the ratios of the lengths of the corresponding sides are not equal:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{12}{8}\\neq\\dfrac{10}{6}\\neq\\dfrac{8}{4}.\n$$\n- $\\mathcal{R}$ is *not* simlar to $\\mathcal{T}$ by SSS since the ratios of the lengths of the corresponding sides are not equal:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{12}{8}\\neq\\dfrac{10}{6}\\neq\\dfrac{9}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$\\mathcal{P}$ only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186124392 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\infty} \\dfrac{3x^4+9x}{6x^4-x^3}.$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\infty} \\dfrac{3x^4+9x}{6x^4-x^3}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186124394 :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow -\\infty} \\dfrac{4-5x-2x^3}{x^2+3}.$" "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow -\\infty} \\dfrac{4-5x-2x^3}{x^2+3}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186124395 :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y=\\lim_\\limits{x \\rightarrow \\infty} f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x^3.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2{x}^{3}+1}{{x}^{3}-15x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{15x}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(2+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(1-\\frac{15}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2+0}{1-0} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote is $y=2.$" "The horizontal asymptote is $$\n\\displaystyle y=\\lim_\\limits{x \\rightarrow \\infty} f(x).\n$$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x^3.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2{x}^{3}+1}{{x}^{3}-15x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{15x}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(2+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}{(1-\\frac{15}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2+0}{1-0} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote is $y=2.$"] [:db/cas 17592186124396 :question/problem "Given the elliptic cone $\\dfrac{(x-2)^2}{4} + \\dfrac{(y-2)^2}{2} = \\dfrac{(z+1)^2}{3},$ find its intercepts with the $z$-axis." "Given the elliptic cone $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{(x-2)^2}{4} + \\dfrac{(y-2)^2}{2} = \\dfrac{(z+1)^2}{3},\n$$ find its intercepts with the $z$-axis."] [:db/cas 17592186124399 :question/problem "What is the trace in the $yz$-plane of the elliptic cylinder $\\dfrac {x^2} 2 +  \\dfrac {(y-2)^2}4  = 1?$" "What is the trace in the $yz$-plane of the elliptic cylinder $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac {x^2} 2 +  \\dfrac {(y-2)^2}4  = 1?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186124408 :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote is $y=\\lim_\\limits{x \\rightarrow \\infty} f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x^3.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{5}-10}{{x}^{3}-8{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{5}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{10}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{8{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{({x}^{2}-\\frac{10}{{x}^{3}})}{(1-\\frac{8}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}-0}{1-0}\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote." "The horizontal asymptote is $$\n\\displaystyle y=\\lim_\\limits{x \\rightarrow \\infty} f(x).\n$$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x^3.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{5}-10}{{x}^{3}-8{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{5}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{10}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{8{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{({x}^{2}-\\frac{10}{{x}^{3}})}{(1-\\frac{8}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}-0}{1-0}\\right) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote."] [:db/cas 17592186124417 :question/worked-solution "Given a $3 \\times 3$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\qquad \\Leftrightarrow \\qquad\n\\left\\{\\;\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{Col} ( M ) = {\\mathbb{R}}^{3} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{dim(Col} ( M ) ) = 3 = \\text{rank} ( M ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{Null} ( M ) = \\{  \\mathbf{0} \\}  \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{dim(Null} ( M ) ) = 0 = \\text{nullity} ( M )\\end{array}} \\right.$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M = A- 7I.$\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that if $\\lambda=7$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(A-7I)\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}$ are eigenvectors of $A,$ which are nonzero by definition. As a result, the matrix $M=A-7 I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $M=A-7I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(A-7I) \\neq 3.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $M=A-7I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Null}(A-7I) \\neq \\{\\mathbf{0}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "Given a $3 \\times 3$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $$\n\\displaystyle \\qquad \\Leftrightarrow \\qquad\n\\left\\{\\;\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{Col} ( M ) = {\\mathbb{R}}^{3} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{dim(Col} ( M ) ) = 3 = \\text{rank} ( M ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{Null} ( M ) = \\{  \\mathbf{0} \\}  \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{dim(Null} ( M ) ) = 0 = \\text{nullity} ( M )\\end{array}} \\right.\n$$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M = A- 7I.$\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that if $\\lambda=7$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(A-7I)\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}$ are eigenvectors of $A,$ which are nonzero by definition. As a result, the matrix $M=A-7 I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $M=A-7I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(A-7I) \\neq 3.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $M=A-7I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Null}(A-7I) \\neq \\{\\mathbf{0}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186124419 :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y, z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overline{AB},$ then there exists a point $C \\in \\overline{AB}$ with position vector $\\mathbf c$ such that\n\n$$\nf (\\mathbf b) − f (\\mathbf a) = \\nabla f (\\mathbf c) · (\\mathbf b − \\mathbf a).\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x, y,z) = x^2 + y - z^2$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overline{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $\\mathbf a = \\langle 0,0,1\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf b= \\langle 2,3,0\\rangle$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,3,0)-f(0,0,1) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨2,3,0⟩-⟨0,0,1⟩) \\\\ 7-(-1) & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,3,-1⟩ \\\\ 8 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,3,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\nabla f$ and evaluating at $\\mathbf c = \\langle c_1, c_2, c_3\\rangle,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨2x,1,-2z⟩, \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨2{c}_{1},1,-2{c}_{3}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,3,-1⟩ \\\\ 8 & =⟨2{c}_{1},1,-2{c}_{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨2,3,-1⟩ \\\\ 8 & =4{c}_{1}+3+2{c}_{3} \\\\ 5 & =4{c}_{1}+2{c}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overline{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-0}{2} & =\\frac{y-0}{3}=\\frac{z-1}{(-1)} \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & =\\frac{y}{3}=1-z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nParametrizing the line, we get\n\n$$\nx = 2t , \\quad y= 3t, \\quad z = 1-t, \\quad 0 \\leq t \\leq 1.\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbf c= \\langle c_1, c_2, c_3\\rangle$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\nc_1 = 2t , \\quad c_2 = 3t , \\quad c_3 = 1-t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the equation $4c_1 + 2c_3 = 5,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(2t)+2(1-t) & =5 \\\\ 8t+2-2t & =5 \\\\ 6t & =3 \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find the values of $c_1,$ $c_2,$ and $c_3.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =2\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=1, \\\\ {c}_{2} & =3\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=\\frac{3}{2}, \\\\ {c}_{3} & =1-\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the coordinates of $C$ are $\\left(1, \\dfrac{3}{2}, \\dfrac{1}{2}\\right).$" "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y, z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overline{AB},$ then there exists a point $C \\in \\overline{AB}$ with position vector $\\mathbf c$ such that\n\n$$\nf (\\mathbf b) − f (\\mathbf a) = \\nabla f (\\mathbf c) · (\\mathbf b − \\mathbf a).\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x, y,z) = x^2 + y - z^2$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overline{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $\\mathbf a = \\langle 0,0,1\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf b= \\langle 2,3,0\\rangle$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,3,0)-f(0,0,1) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨2,3,0⟩-⟨0,0,1⟩) \\\\ 7-(-1) & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,3,-1⟩ \\\\ 8 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,3,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\nabla f$ and evaluating at $\\mathbf c = \\langle c_1, c_2, c_3\\rangle,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨2x,1,-2z⟩, \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨2{c}_{1},1,-2{c}_{3}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,3,-1⟩ \\\\ 8 & =⟨2{c}_{1},1,-2{c}_{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨2,3,-1⟩ \\\\ 8 & =4{c}_{1}+3+2{c}_{3} \\\\ 5 & =4{c}_{1}+2{c}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overline{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-0}{2} & =\\frac{y-0}{3}=\\frac{z-1}{(-1)} \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & =\\frac{y}{3}=1-z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nParametrizing the line, we get\n\n$$\nx = 2t , \\quad y= 3t, \\quad z = 1-t, \\quad 0 \\leq t \\leq 1.\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbf c= \\langle c_1, c_2, c_3\\rangle$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\nc_1 = 2t , \\quad c_2 = 3t , \\quad c_3 = 1-t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the equation $4c_1 + 2c_3 = 5,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(2t)+2(1-t) & =5 \\\\ 8t+2-2t & =5 \\\\ 6t & =3 \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find the values of $c_1,$ $c_2,$ and $c_3.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =2\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=1, \\\\ {c}_{2} & =3\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=\\frac{3}{2}, \\\\ {c}_{3} & =1-\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the coordinates of $C$ are $$\n\\displaystyle \\left(1, \\dfrac{3}{2}, \\dfrac{1}{2}\\right).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186124435 :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathbf{T}$ is a linear transformation from $\\mathbb{R}^3$ into $\\mathbb{R}^3,$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $\\mathbf{T}$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad\n\\left\\{\\;\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{Im} ( \\mathbf{T} ) = {\\mathbb{R}}^{3} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{rank} ( \\mathbf{T} ) = 3 \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{Ker} ( \\mathbf{T} ) = \\{  \\mathbf{0} \\}  \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{nullity} ( \\mathbf{T} ) = 0\\end{array}} \\right.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $[1, 2, 1]^T \\not\\in  \\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T}),$ we must have that $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T}) \\neq \\mathbb{R}^3.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since the vector $[1, 2, 1]^T$ does not lie in $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})$, then $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T}) \\neq \\mathbb{R}^3.$ Therefore, according to the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\textrm{Ker}(\\mathbf{T}) \\neq \\{\\mathbf{0}\\}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T}) \\neq \\mathbb{R}^3,$ the transformation $\\mathbf T$ can't be invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "Since $\\mathbf{T}$ is a linear transformation from $\\mathbb{R}^3$ into $\\mathbb{R}^3,$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $\\mathbf{T}$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $$\n\\displaystyle \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad\n\\left\\{\\;\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{Im} ( \\mathbf{T} ) = {\\mathbb{R}}^{3} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{rank} ( \\mathbf{T} ) = 3 \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{Ker} ( \\mathbf{T} ) = \\{  \\mathbf{0} \\}  \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{nullity} ( \\mathbf{T} ) = 0\\end{array}} \\right.\n$$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $[1, 2, 1]^T \\not\\in  \\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T}),$ we must have that $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T}) \\neq \\mathbb{R}^3.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since the vector $[1, 2, 1]^T$ does not lie in $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})$, then $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T}) \\neq \\mathbb{R}^3.$ Therefore, according to the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\textrm{Ker}(\\mathbf{T}) \\neq \\{\\mathbf{0}\\}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T}) \\neq \\mathbb{R}^3,$ the transformation $\\mathbf T$ can't be invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186124436 :question/problem "$\\mathbf{T}$ is a bijection $\\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad\n\\left\\{\\;\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\bbox[3pt,white,border:2px solid Silver]{\\phantom{W W}} = {\\mathbb{R}}^{n} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{rank} ( \\mathbf{T} ) = n \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{Ker} ( \\mathbf{T} ) = \\{  \\mathbf{0} \\}  \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{nullity} ( \\mathbf{T} ) = 0\\end{array}} \\right.$ \n$\\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad$  $T$ is invertible\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $\\mathbf{T}:\\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}^n$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ What should be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?" "$\\mathbf{T}$ is a bijection $$\n\\displaystyle \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad\n\\left\\{\\;\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\bbox[3pt,white,border:2px solid Silver]{\\phantom{W W}} = {\\mathbb{R}}^{n} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{rank} ( \\mathbf{T} ) = n \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{Ker} ( \\mathbf{T} ) = \\{  \\mathbf{0} \\}  \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{nullity} ( \\mathbf{T} ) = 0\\end{array}} \\right.\n$$ \n$\\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad$  $T$ is invertible\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $\\mathbf{T}:\\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}^n$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ What should be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?"] [:db/cas 17592186124436 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the *invertible matrix theorem* (in terms of linear transformations) states the following: \n\n $\\mathbf{T}$ is a bijection $\\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad\n\\left\\{\\;\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\bbox[3pt,white,border:2px solid Silver]{ {\\color{blue}\\text{Im} ( \\mathbf{T} )}} = {\\mathbb{R}}^{n} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{rank} ( \\mathbf{T} ) = n \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{Ker} ( \\mathbf{T} ) = \\{  \\mathbf{0} \\}  \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{nullity} ( \\mathbf{T} ) = 0\\end{array}} \\right.$ \n$\\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad$  $T$ is invertible\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T}).$" "Recall that the *invertible matrix theorem* (in terms of linear transformations) states the following: \n\n $\\mathbf{T}$ is a bijection $$\n\\displaystyle \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad\n\\left\\{\\;\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\bbox[3pt,white,border:2px solid Silver]{ {\\color{blue}\\text{Im} ( \\mathbf{T} )}} = {\\mathbb{R}}^{n} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{rank} ( \\mathbf{T} ) = n \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{Ker} ( \\mathbf{T} ) = \\{  \\mathbf{0} \\}  \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{nullity} ( \\mathbf{T} ) = 0\\end{array}} \\right.\n$$ \n$\\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow \\qquad$  $T$ is invertible\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T}).$"] [:db/cas 17592186124760 :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that the function $f(x,y)=2{x}^{3}-y$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,1⟩$ and $b=⟨2,2⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,2)-f(0,1) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨2,2⟩-⟨0,1⟩) \\\\ (2(2)^{3}-2)-(2(0)^{3}-1) & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,1⟩ \\\\ 15 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨6{x}^{2},-1⟩, \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨6{c}_{1}^{2},-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15 & =∇f⟨c⟩\\cdot ⟨2,1⟩ \\\\ 15 & =⟨6{c}_{1}^{2},-1⟩\\cdot ⟨2,1⟩ \\\\ 15 & =12{c}_{1}^{2}-1 \\\\ 16 & =12{c}_{1}^{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}^{2} & =\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ {c}_{1} & =\\pm \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}<0<2,$ it's clear that $-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}$ corresponds to the $x$-coordinate of a point that does not lie on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ Therefore, we can ignore it. Thus, ${c}_{1}=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}.$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}$ to find ${c}_{2}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-{y}_{a}}{{y}_{b}-{y}_{a}} & =\\frac{x-{x}_{a}}{{x}_{b}-{x}_{a}} \\\\ \\frac{y-1}{2-1} & =\\frac{x-0}{2-0} \\\\ y-1 & =\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ y & =\\frac{x}{2}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} & =\\frac{{c}_{1}}{2}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking into account that ${c}_{1}=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}},$ we obtain that ${c}_{2}=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{2}+1=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}+1.$ So, the point $C$ that satisfies the mean value theorem is $(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}+1).$" "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that the function $f(x,y)=2{x}^{3}-y$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,1⟩$ and $b=⟨2,2⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,2)-f(0,1) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨2,2⟩-⟨0,1⟩) \\\\ (2(2)^{3}-2)-(2(0)^{3}-1) & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,1⟩ \\\\ 15 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨6{x}^{2},-1⟩, \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨6{c}_{1}^{2},-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15 & =∇f⟨c⟩\\cdot ⟨2,1⟩ \\\\ 15 & =⟨6{c}_{1}^{2},-1⟩\\cdot ⟨2,1⟩ \\\\ 15 & =12{c}_{1}^{2}-1 \\\\ 16 & =12{c}_{1}^{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}^{2} & =\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ {c}_{1} & =\\pm \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}<0<2,$ it's clear that $-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}$ corresponds to the $x$-coordinate of a point that does not lie on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ Therefore, we can ignore it. Thus, ${c}_{1}=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}.$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}$ to find ${c}_{2}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-{y}_{a}}{{y}_{b}-{y}_{a}} & =\\frac{x-{x}_{a}}{{x}_{b}-{x}_{a}} \\\\ \\frac{y-1}{2-1} & =\\frac{x-0}{2-0} \\\\ y-1 & =\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ y & =\\frac{x}{2}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} & =\\frac{{c}_{1}}{2}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking into account that ${c}_{1}=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}},$ we obtain that $$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{2}+1=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}+1.\n$$ So, the point $C$ that satisfies the mean value theorem is $$\n\\displaystyle (\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}+1).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186124765 :question/problem "Let $D$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ matrices of the form $[\\begin{aligned}a & 0 \\\\ 0 & a\\end{aligned}],$ where $a\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?  \n\nI. The identity matrix ${I}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to $D.$\n\nII. For the zero matrix $O$ and any $A,B\\in D,$ we have $A+O=O.$\n\nIII. For any $A,B\\in D,$ we have $A+B=B+A.$" "Let $D$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ matrices of the form $$\n\\displaystyle [\\begin{aligned}a & 0 \\\\ 0 & a\\end{aligned}],\n$$ where $a\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?  \n\nI. The identity matrix $$\n\\displaystyle {I}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$ belongs to $D.$\n\nII. For the zero matrix $O$ and any $A,B\\in D,$ we have $A+O=O.$\n\nIII. For any $A,B\\in D,$ we have $A+B=B+A.$"] [:db/cas 17592186124765 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, every element $A$ in $D$ satisfies ${a}_{12}={a}_{21}=0$ and ${a}_{11}={a}_{22}.$ So, ${I}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\in D.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, consider the identity matrix ${I}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\in D.$ Then\n\n$$\n{I}_{2}+O=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\ne O.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, For any matrices $A,B\\in D,$ we obtain that \n\n$$\nA+B=B+A\n$$\n\nsince matrix addition is commutative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, every element $A$ in $D$ satisfies ${a}_{12}={a}_{21}=0$ and ${a}_{11}={a}_{22}.$ So, $$\n\\displaystyle {I}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\in D.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, consider the identity matrix $$\n\\displaystyle {I}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\in D.\n$$ Then\n\n$$\n{I}_{2}+O=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\ne O.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, For any matrices $A,B\\in D,$ we obtain that \n\n$$\nA+B=B+A\n$$\n\nsince matrix addition is commutative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186124772 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq & =q \\\\ Pq-q & =0 \\\\ Pq-Iq & =0 \\\\ (P-I)q & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=\\begin{bmatrix}0.4 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.6 & 0.9\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-0.6 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.6 & -0.1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.6 & 0.1 & 0 \\\\ 0.6 & -0.1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-0.6 & 0.1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{7}{6}{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =\\frac{6}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{7} \\\\ \\frac{6}{7}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $a-b=\\frac{1}{7}-\\frac{6}{7}=-\\frac{5}{7}.$" "Recall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq & =q \\\\ Pq-q & =0 \\\\ Pq-Iq & =0 \\\\ (P-I)q & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=\\begin{bmatrix}0.4 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.6 & 0.9\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-0.6 & 0.1 \\\\ 0.6 & -0.1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.6 & 0.1 & 0 \\\\ 0.6 & -0.1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-0.6 & 0.1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{7}{6}{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =\\frac{6}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{7} \\\\ \\frac{6}{7}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $$\n\\displaystyle a-b=\\frac{1}{7}-\\frac{6}{7}=-\\frac{5}{7}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186124773 :question/problem "$P = \\begin{bmatrix}0.9 & 0.2 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.2 & 0.1 \\\\ 0 & 0.6 & 0.6\\end{bmatrix}, q = \\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nGiven that $q$ is the steady-state vector of the stochastic matrix $P$ shown above, what is the value of $a + b - c$?" "$$\n\\displaystyle P = \\begin{bmatrix}0.9 & 0.2 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.1 & 0.2 & 0.1 \\\\ 0 & 0.6 & 0.6\\end{bmatrix}, q = \\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nGiven that $q$ is the steady-state vector of the stochastic matrix $P$ shown above, what is the value of $a + b - c$?"] [:db/cas 17592186124790 :question/problem "> $T$ is a bijection $\\hspace{2em} \\Longleftrightarrow  \\hspace{2em} \\{  \\hspace{0.278em} \\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{Im} ( \\mathbf{T} ) = {\\mathbb{R}}^{n} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{rank} ( \\mathbf{T} ) = n \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{Ker} ( \\mathbf{T} ) = \\bbox[3pt,white,border:2px solid Gainsboro]{\\phantom{W}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{nullity} ( \\mathbf{T} ) = 0\\end{array}$\n> $⟺$ $T$ is invertible\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $T: R^{n} → R^{n}$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T$. Which of the following should be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?" "> $T$ is a bijection $$\n\\displaystyle \\hspace{2em} \\Longleftrightarrow  \\hspace{2em} \\{  \\hspace{0.278em} \\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{Im} ( \\mathbf{T} ) = {\\mathbb{R}}^{n} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{rank} ( \\mathbf{T} ) = n \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{Ker} ( \\mathbf{T} ) = \\bbox[3pt,white,border:2px solid Gainsboro]{\\phantom{W}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{nullity} ( \\mathbf{T} ) = 0\\end{array}\n$$\n> $⟺$ $T$ is invertible\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $T: R^{n} → R^{n}$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T$. Which of the following should be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?"] [:db/cas 17592186124828 :question/problem "What is the domain in the $xy$-plane of the hyperbolic cylinder $\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{2}=1?$" "What is the domain in the $xy$-plane of the hyperbolic cylinder $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{2}=1?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186124854 :question/problem "Consider the straight line given by the following parametric equations:\n$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x = 3 - 5 t \\\\ y = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} + 4 t \\\\ z = 1 - 2 t\\end{array} \\right.$\nWhat would be the vector equation of the line?" "Consider the straight line given by the following parametric equations:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x = 3 - 5 t \\\\ y = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} + 4 t \\\\ z = 1 - 2 t\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\nWhat would be the vector equation of the line?"] [:db/cas 17592186124855 :question/problem "Consider the straight line given by the following parametric equations:\n$\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x = - 2 + 6 t \\\\ y = 4 t \\\\ z = 3 - t\\end{array} \\right.$\nFind a vector that is parallel to the line." "Consider the straight line given by the following parametric equations:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{l}x = - 2 + 6 t \\\\ y = 4 t \\\\ z = 3 - t\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\nFind a vector that is parallel to the line."] [:db/cas 17592186124863 :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\text{Col}(A) &={R}^{5} \\\\ \\text{dim(Col}(A)) &=5=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\ \\text{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) &=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $\\text{dim(Null}(A))=0$ is false. Since \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\{{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\,|\\,{x}_{1}\\in R\\}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(A))=1\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement “$A$ is not invertible” is true. Since $\\text{dim(Null}(A))\\ne 0,$ then according to the invertible matrix theorem, the matrix $A$ can't be invertible.\n\n- Statement $\\text{rank}(A)=5$ is false. Since $A$ is not invertible, then by the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\text{rank}(A)\\ne 5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$A$ is not invertible” only." "Since $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\text{Col}(A) &={R}^{5} \\\\ \\text{dim(Col}(A)) &=5=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\ \\text{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) &=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $\\text{dim(Null}(A))=0$ is false. Since \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\{{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\,|\\,{x}_{1}\\in R\\}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(A))=1\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement “$A$ is not invertible” is true. Since $\\text{dim(Null}(A))\\ne 0,$ then according to the invertible matrix theorem, the matrix $A$ can't be invertible.\n\n- Statement $\\text{rank}(A)=5$ is false. Since $A$ is not invertible, then by the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\text{rank}(A)\\ne 5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$A$ is not invertible” only."] [:db/cas 17592186124871 :question/problem "Given $D=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 8 & 0 & 10 & 9 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 8 & -4 & 2 & -4\\end{bmatrix},\\,$ what is $\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(D))?$" "Given $$\n\\displaystyle D=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 8 & 0 & 10 & 9 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 8 & -4 & 2 & -4\\end{bmatrix},\\,\n$$ what is $\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(D))?$"] [:db/cas 17592186124878 :question/problem "What is the trace in the $xz$-plane of the hyperbolic cylinder $\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{8}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1?$" "What is the trace in the $xz$-plane of the hyperbolic cylinder $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{8}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186124881 :question/problem "What is the trace in the $yz$-plane of the hyperboloid of one sheet $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{5}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{3}=1?$" "What is the trace in the $yz$-plane of the hyperboloid of one sheet $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{5}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{3}=1?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186124882 :question/problem "The projection of the intersection between the hyperboloid of one sheet $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{18}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{50}=1$ and the plane $x=2$ onto the $yz$-plane is a hyperbola. What are the lengths of the semi-axes of the hyperbola?" "The projection of the intersection between the hyperboloid of one sheet $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{18}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{50}=1\n$$ and the plane $x=2$ onto the $yz$-plane is a hyperbola. What are the lengths of the semi-axes of the hyperbola?"] [:db/cas 17592186124883 :question/problem "Given the hyperboloid of one sheet $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{5}=1$, find its $x$-intercepts." "Given the hyperboloid of one sheet $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{5}=1,\n$$ find its $x$-intercepts."] [:db/cas 17592186124886 :question/problem "Given the ellipsoid $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{12}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{6}+\\frac{(z-1)^{2}}{2}=1$, find its intercepts with the $x$-axis." "Given the ellipsoid $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{12}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{6}+\\frac{(z-1)^{2}}{2}=1,\n$$ find its intercepts with the $x$-axis."]]
